  </button><script>
// ===================================================
// FAQ DATA — Tradovate
// ===================================================
const FAQ_DATA = [
  {
    category: "TradingView Automation & PickMyTrade",
    icon: "&#9889;",
    description: "Webhook setup, execution speed, and multi-account routing",
    questions: [
      { q: "How do I connect TradingView to Tradovate using PickMyTrade?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Create a PickMyTrade account, link your Tradovate login via OAuth, copy the webhook URL PickMyTrade gives you, and paste it into your TradingView alert's webhook field. Your alert message needs to be JSON formatted so PickMyTrade knows the symbol, side, and quantity. Full setup typically takes about 5 minutes and requires no coding.</p>" },
      { q: "Does PickMyTrade work with Tradovate natively?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate is one of PickMyTrade's core supported brokers alongside Rithmic, Interactive Brokers, TradeStation, and others. PickMyTrade costs $50/month flat with unlimited alerts, strategies, and connected accounts.</p>" },
      { q: "How fast is PickMyTrade execution to Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>PickMyTrade advertises sub-200ms execution from TradingView alert fire to Tradovate order placement under normal conditions. Actual speed varies with TradingView alert latency, your internet connection, and Tradovate API load during news events.</p>" },
      { q: "Do I need TradingView Premium to automate Tradovate via webhook?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>You need at least TradingView's Essential (formerly Pro) plan because webhooks are not included in the free tier. Pro+ or Premium gives you more alerts and longer server-side alert expirations, which matters if you run many strategies.</p>" },
      { q: "Why does my Tradovate alert not fire from TradingView?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Most common causes: the TradingView alert is not actually active, your Tradovate session expired, the JSON in the alert message is malformed, or you hit TradingView's webhook rate limit. Check the webhook log inside PickMyTrade first — it tells you whether the alert arrived or not.</p>" },
      { q: "What JSON format does a Tradovate webhook alert need?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>At minimum the JSON needs a symbol, a side (buy/sell), and a quantity, plus the specific account it should target. PickMyTrade gives you a template you can copy into your TradingView alert message with placeholders like <code>{{strategy.order.action}}</code> and <code>{{strategy.order.contracts}}</code>.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I automate a TradingView strategy (not just an indicator) on Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — TradingView strategies expose placeholders like <code>{{strategy.order.action}}</code>, <code>{{strategy.order.contracts}}</code>, and <code>{{strategy.order.price}}</code> that fire on each backtested signal when the strategy runs live. PickMyTrade parses these and routes each entry/exit to Tradovate.</p>" },
      { q: "Does PickMyTrade support stop loss and take profit brackets on Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — you can include SL and TP values directly in the JSON payload or configure them as defaults inside PickMyTrade. Brackets are sent as OCO orders to Tradovate so the TP cancels when the SL fires and vice versa.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I automate multiple Tradovate accounts with one TradingView alert?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — PickMyTrade supports multi-account copying so one alert fires across every linked Tradovate account you list in the payload. This is the typical setup for traders running multiple prop firm evaluations or combines at once.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I set different contract sizes per account in PickMyTrade?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>PickMyTrade supports a quantity multiplier per linked account, so if your base alert is 1 contract you can scale account A to 3x and account B to 5x from the dashboard. This is useful when you have mixed account sizes at different prop firms.</p>" },
      { q: "Why does PickMyTrade need my Tradovate login?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Tradovate's API uses username/password plus a device token to issue access tokens, so automation platforms need those credentials to place orders on your behalf. PickMyTrade stores them encrypted and only uses them to maintain the API session.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I test the automation on a Tradovate demo/sim account first?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate has a free simulation environment, and PickMyTrade lets you pick the sim account when you fire webhooks. Most traders run their strategy on sim for a week or two before pointing the webhook at a live or funded account.</p>" },
      { q: "Does PickMyTrade handle partial fills on Tradovate?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Yes — if Tradovate only fills part of a market or limit order, PickMyTrade reports the actual filled quantity back so brackets attach to the real position, not the intended one. This matters most in low-liquidity sessions like Sunday open.</p>" },
      { q: "Can PickMyTrade reverse or close a Tradovate position with one alert?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — you can send a 'close' or 'flat' action in your JSON payload and PickMyTrade will cancel any working bracket orders and flatten the position. Some traders bind this to a kill-switch alert they fire manually if the strategy misbehaves.</p>" },
      { q: "Do I need a VPS to run TradingView to Tradovate automation?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>No — TradingView alerts fire from TradingView's servers and PickMyTrade runs in the cloud, so nothing runs on your local machine. A VPS only matters if you're running a desktop platform like NinjaTrader as the signal source.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I use Pine Script indicators (not strategies) to trigger Tradovate automation?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — you set <code>alertcondition()</code> triggers on an indicator and include the JSON action (buy/sell/close) in the alert message itself, since indicators don't expose strategy placeholders. This is the common path for discretionary-style setups.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between a Pine Script strategy and indicator for webhook automation?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Pine Script strategies and indicators are fundamentally different script types</strong> on TradingView, and the distinction matters for webhook automation.</p><p><strong>Indicators</strong> use the <code>alertcondition()</code> function to trigger alerts based on custom conditions. They paint visual elements on charts (lines, labels) and generate alerts when conditions are met. You define the alert message manually, including the JSON payload for your webhook.</p><p><strong>Strategies</strong> use <code>strategy.entry()</code>, <code>strategy.close()</code>, and related functions to simulate trades in TradingView's Strategy Tester. They automatically generate order-fill alerts without needing <code>alertcondition()</code> — whenever the strategy opens, closes, or reverses a position, an alert fires.</p><p><strong>Which is better for automation?</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Strategies (recommended):</strong> Alerts are tied to actual simulated trade actions (entry, exit, reversal), making signal-to-trade mapping cleaner. You also get built-in backtesting to validate performance before going live.</li><li><strong>Indicators:</strong> More flexibility in defining complex alert conditions, but require manual coordination between alert logic and trade actions.</li></ul><p>For PickMyTrade webhook automation, both work. Set up a webhook alert, configure the JSON message with action, symbol, and quantity fields, and PickMyTrade sends the trade to your broker. Most PickMyTrade users choose strategies for the backtesting advantage and cleaner alert flow.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Account Setup & Eligibility",
    icon: "&#128221;",
    description: "Opening an account, KYC, age, residency, and documents",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is the minimum age to open a Tradovate account?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Tradovate requires account holders to be 18 or older. This is standard across US futures brokers because of NFA/CFTC rules around futures suitability.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I open a Tradovate account outside the United States?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate accepts accounts from many countries, though specific eligibility depends on where you live and they ask you to email support to confirm. One known exception: Canadian residents typically cannot open a live Tradovate account directly and usually access Tradovate through a prop firm like Apex instead.</p>" },
      { q: "What documents do I need to open a Tradovate account?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Tradovate's KYC process requires a government-issued photo ID (driver's license, passport, or state ID) and proof of residence like a utility bill or bank statement dated within the last 90 days. US citizens also need to provide their Social Security number.</p>" },
      { q: "How long does Tradovate account approval take?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Most applications are reviewed within 1–3 business days once you've submitted all required KYC documents. Funding with ACH then adds a 10-day holding period before those deposited funds become tradeable for withdrawal purposes.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the minimum deposit to fund a live Tradovate account?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Tradovate's published account minimum is typically $250 to open, but practical day trading with reduced intraday margin generally needs $2,500 or more to have cushion on a single contract. Check current Tradovate funding guidance because minimums can shift.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I have a Tradovate account and a prop firm Tradovate account at the same time?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — a personal Tradovate account and a prop firm Tradovate account are separate logins and can coexist. Many traders keep a small personal account for testing and a funded prop account for scaled trading.</p>" },
      { q: "Do I need a separate demo account or does my live login include sim?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A live Tradovate account automatically includes a simulation environment — at the login screen you pick 'Live' or 'Simulation'. A standalone demo (with no live account attached) is also available but can be disabled after a period of inactivity.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I switch between live and demo on Tradovate?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Log out and on the login screen pick either 'Live Environment' or 'Simulation Environment' — these are separate session types. If you only see one option, it's because your current login is limited to that environment.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Pricing, Fees & Commissions",
    icon: "&#128176;",
    description: "Plans, per-contract costs, data fees, and liquidation fees",
    questions: [
      { q: "How does Tradovate's pricing work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Tradovate offers three plans: a <strong>Free plan</strong> (no monthly fee but higher per-contract commissions), a <strong>Monthly plan</strong> (typically $99/month with lower commissions), and a <strong>Lifetime plan</strong> (a one-time payment, commonly $1,499, with the lowest commissions). Check current Tradovate pricing because tier prices and commission rates change.</p>" },
      { q: "What are Tradovate's per-contract commission rates?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>As of early 2026, commissions are roughly $1.29 per side on the Free plan, around $0.99 per side on the Monthly plan, and around $0.59 per side on the Lifetime plan for standard contracts, with lower rates on micros. These are the Tradovate commission only — exchange, NFA, and clearing fees stack on top.</p>" },
      { q: "What are the 'all-in' costs per futures trade on Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>All-in means Tradovate's commission plus the CME exchange fee, clearing fee, and NFA fee combined. For an E-mini like ES, all-in costs typically land a few dollars per side depending on your plan — Tradovate publishes an all-in rate breakdown you should check before choosing a plan.</p>" },
      { q: "Does Tradovate charge platform or data fees on top of commissions?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The platform itself (web, desktop, mobile) is included, but real-time market data is separate. Non-professional traders pay around $12/month for the CME Group Level I bundle or around $41/month for Level II across CME, CBOT, NYMEX, and COMEX.</p>" },
      { q: "Are professional trader data fees higher on Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — professional users pay around $156/month per exchange for market data, following CME's professional-user pricing schedule. Whether you're classified professional depends on how you use the data (licensed trader, firm employee, etc.), not just volume.</p>" },
      { q: "Is there a liquidation fee on Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate charges a <strong>$50 liquidation fee</strong> when the system auto-liquidates your account for violating risk settings or going below margin. Multiple users on Trustpilot have flagged this fee because most competing brokers don't charge one.</p>" },
      { q: "Which Tradovate plan is cheapest for my volume?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>As a rough break-even: the Monthly plan usually pays off above roughly 300 sides per month vs the Free plan, and the Lifetime plan pays off over time if you trade consistently and high-volume for a year or more. Do your own math against the current Tradovate rate card before upgrading.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the Tradovate API add-on fee and CME Individual License Agreement (ILA), and how do I avoid paying them?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Tradovate charges an <strong>API Access add-on fee</strong> if you want to connect custom-built trading applications directly to the Tradovate API. This fee has historically been $150+/month and is separate from your regular commission plan. Additionally, any application that connects to CME Group market data through the Tradovate API must comply with the <strong>CME Individual License Agreement (ILA)</strong> — a legal agreement governing redistribution and display of CME data.</p><p>For most traders, these costs and compliance requirements make DIY API automation impractical:</p><ul><li><strong>API add-on fee:</strong> Charged monthly on top of your existing Tradovate plan. This alone exceeds the cost of most third-party automation services.</li><li><strong>CME ILA:</strong> Requires you to register as a data vendor, comply with reporting obligations, and potentially pay additional data fees depending on how your application uses CME market data.</li><li><strong>Development time:</strong> Building, testing, and maintaining your own API integration requires significant programming expertise.</li></ul><p><strong>How to avoid these costs:</strong> Third-party automation platforms like PickMyTrade handle the API connection, CME compliance, and infrastructure for you. PickMyTrade's $50/month flat fee includes unlimited Tradovate automation with no API add-on charges and no CME ILA paperwork.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Platform Features",
    icon: "&#128241;",
    description: "Cloud access, mobile app, order types, charting, and API",
    questions: [
      { q: "Is Tradovate a cloud-based platform?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate was one of the first fully cloud-based futures brokers, which means your charts, orders, and settings sync across web, desktop, iPad, iPhone, and Android. You do not have to install heavy local software like some competitors require.</p>" },
      { q: "Does Tradovate have a mobile app?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate has iOS and Android apps where you can view charts, DOM, and place bracket orders directly from the chart or order ladder. It's a real trading app, not a read-only dashboard.</p>" },
      { q: "What order types does Tradovate support?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Market, Limit, Stop, Stop-Limit, MIT (Market if Touched), LIT (Limit if Touched), and bracket orders including OSO (One-Sends-Other) and OCO (One-Cancels-Other). Brackets can be attached at entry so your SL and TP go live the moment you're filled.</p>" },
      { q: "Does Tradovate offer charting with indicators?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate ships with 40+ built-in indicators and multiple chart types including candles, Heikin Ashi, Renko, and Point &amp; Figure. You can also build and share custom indicators through their developer repo.</p>" },
      { q: "Does Tradovate have an API?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate exposes a REST + WebSocket API documented at api.tradovate.com that covers authentication, orders, positions, and market data. Most webhook automation tools (PickMyTrade included) use this API under the hood.</p>" },
      { q: "Does Tradovate integrate directly with TradingView?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate is a TradingView broker partner, so you can trade live from TradingView charts directly. That built-in integration is separate from webhook-based automation, which goes through third-party platforms like PickMyTrade.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I use Tradovate with NinjaTrader?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — since NinjaTrader owns Tradovate (since 2022), NinjaTrader 8 can connect to a Tradovate account as a data/execution source. You download NinjaTrader, log in with your Tradovate credentials, and trade.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I roll a futures contract on Tradovate — does it happen automatically?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Tradovate auto-rolls your charts by default</strong>, but it does <em>not</em> roll open positions. When a futures contract approaches expiration, Tradovate's 'AutoRoll' feature automatically switches your charts and workspace modules to the next front-month contract — for example, from ESH6 (March) to ESM6 (June).</p><p>However, if you hold an open position in the expiring contract, you must manually close it and re-open in the new contract month. AutoRoll updates your <em>charts</em>, not your <em>trades</em>.</p><ul><li><strong>To disable AutoRoll:</strong> Go to Settings → enable 'Prohibit Autoroll Contracts.' You can then manually roll via the gear icon in chart or SuperDOM modules.</li><li><strong>Mobile users:</strong> Initiate rollovers through the Markets menu.</li><li><strong>Automation tip:</strong> If you use PickMyTrade to automate TradingView alerts on Tradovate, ensure your TradingView chart uses the correct front-month contract month symbol (e.g., ESU2026, MNQZ2026). PickMyTrade sends the exact symbol shown on your chart to Tradovate — for continuous symbols (ES1!, MNQ1!), PickMyTrade automatically maps them to the current front-month tradeable contract.</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What are Tradovate's daily trading hours and the CME maintenance window?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Tradovate follows CME Globex hours</strong> for most US futures products. The trading session runs from <strong>Sunday 5:00 PM to Friday 4:00 PM Central Time (CT)</strong>, with a daily one-hour maintenance break.</p><ul><li><strong>Daily maintenance window:</strong> 4:00 PM – 5:00 PM CT (5:00 PM – 6:00 PM ET), Monday through Thursday. No orders can be placed or executed during this hour.</li><li><strong>Pre-close rule:</strong> At 3:45 PM CT (15 min before close), Tradovate requires you to be flat or hold full exchange margin to avoid liquidation.</li><li><strong>Queued orders:</strong> Orders submitted during maintenance execute at the 5:00 PM CT reopen — at the opening price, which may gap from your expected level.</li><li><strong>Weekend:</strong> Markets close Friday 4:00 PM CT and reopen Sunday 5:00 PM CT.</li></ul><p>If you automate trading through PickMyTrade, alerts firing during the maintenance window are queued and processed at reopen. Configure your TradingView strategy to avoid signals during the 4:00–5:00 PM CT break to prevent unexpected gap fills.</p>" },
      { q: "What are the tick sizes and point values for popular futures contracts on Tradovate (ES, NQ, MES, MNQ)?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Understanding tick sizes and point values is essential</strong> before trading futures on Tradovate. Here are the most commonly traded contracts:</p><ul><li><strong>ES (E-mini S&amp;P 500):</strong> Tick size = 0.25 points, tick value = $12.50, 1 point = $50</li><li><strong>MES (Micro E-mini S&amp;P 500):</strong> Tick size = 0.25 points, tick value = $1.25, 1 point = $5</li><li><strong>NQ (E-mini Nasdaq-100):</strong> Tick size = 0.25 points, tick value = $5.00, 1 point = $20</li><li><strong>MNQ (Micro E-mini Nasdaq-100):</strong> Tick size = 0.25 points, tick value = $0.50, 1 point = $2</li><li><strong>YM (E-mini Dow):</strong> Tick size = 1 point, tick value = $5.00</li><li><strong>CL (Crude Oil):</strong> Tick size = 0.01, tick value = $10.00</li><li><strong>GC (Gold):</strong> Tick size = 0.10, tick value = $10.00</li></ul><p>Micro contracts (MES, MNQ) are 1/10th the size of their full-size counterparts — ideal for beginners and small accounts. A 10-point Nasdaq move means $200 on NQ but only $20 on MNQ.</p><p>When setting stop-loss and take-profit values in PickMyTrade or TradingView alerts for Tradovate, always specify values in <strong>points</strong> (not ticks) to ensure correct order placement.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a continuous contract on Tradovate and can I place orders from it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Continuous contracts on Tradovate</strong> (like @ES or NQ1!) are synthetic symbols that automatically represent the current front-month contract. They stitch together historical data from successive contract months, giving you a seamless price chart without gaps at expiration.</p><ul><li><strong>Charting only:</strong> Continuous contracts are for charting and technical analysis — drawing trendlines, calculating moving averages, and backtesting strategies.</li><li><strong>No direct orders:</strong> You cannot place orders on a continuous contract symbol in Tradovate. The platform routes your order to the actual front-month contract (e.g., ESM6 for June 2026).</li><li><strong>Price data:</strong> The current price displayed on a continuous contract chart matches the front-month contract, which typically has the highest volume and liquidity.</li></ul><p>For TradingView automation: continuous contract symbols (e.g., CME_MINI:ES1!) are fully supported — PickMyTrade automatically maps them to the current front-month tradeable contract on Tradovate and handles rollover approximately four days before expiration. If you want to trade a specific contract month instead of the auto-mapped front month, simply enter that exact symbol (e.g., ESU2026) when generating your alert in PickMyTrade's <strong>Generate Alert</strong> section.</p>" },
      { q: "When does Tradovate observe CME holiday trading hours, and where can I find the schedule?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Tradovate follows CME Group's official holiday schedule</strong>, which includes early closes or full closures on major US holidays.</p><ul><li><strong>Full closures:</strong> New Year's Day, Good Friday, Christmas Day</li><li><strong>Early closes (typically 12:00 PM CT):</strong> MLK Day, Presidents' Day, Memorial Day, Juneteenth, Independence Day, Labor Day, Thanksgiving (and day after)</li></ul><p>On early-close days, most CME products halt trading at <strong>12:00 PM CT</strong> instead of the regular 4:00 PM CT. The market typically reopens at its normal 5:00 PM CT time unless the following day is also a holiday.</p><p><strong>Where to find the current schedule:</strong></p><ul><li>Tradovate publishes the holiday schedule on their support page each year</li><li>CME Group's official schedule at cmegroup.com/trading-hours</li></ul><p>If you run automated strategies through PickMyTrade, be aware that holiday sessions often have lower liquidity, wider spreads, and the potential for early flattening by your broker or prop firm. Consider pausing automation on major holidays.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Funding & Withdrawals",
    icon: "&#127974;",
    description: "ACH, wire transfers, limits, and processing timelines",
    questions: [
      { q: "How do I fund a Tradovate account?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Tradovate accepts ACH bank transfer, domestic wire, and international wire. Debit card funding is not supported the way retail stock brokers offer it — futures regulation effectively limits funding to bank methods.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the ACH deposit limit on Tradovate?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>ACH deposits are capped at around $5,000 per transaction, and deposits submitted before 1:30 PM Central typically post the same business day. Multiple smaller ACH deposits are allowed if you need more than $5,000 in the account.</p>" },
      { q: "How long until my Tradovate deposit is tradeable?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Wire transfers typically post within a few hours to the next business day once received. ACH deposits usually post quickly but are subject to a 10 calendar day hold before those funds can be withdrawn again.</p>" },
      { q: "How do withdrawals from Tradovate work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>You request a withdrawal in the account module and Tradovate processes it by ACH or wire. Wires typically clear within one business day, ACH can take a bit longer, and you can't withdraw funds below the account minimum if you still have open positions.</p>" },
      { q: "Are there withdrawal fees on Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>ACH withdrawals are typically free, while wire withdrawals usually incur a fee charged by Tradovate plus any receiving bank fee. Check the current fee schedule in your account because specific fee amounts change.</p>" },
      { q: "What currencies does Tradovate accept for funding?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Tradovate accounts are USD-denominated and funding is primarily accepted in USD. Non-USD deposits typically get converted by the sending bank and any FX fee comes from that bank, not Tradovate.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Security & Regulation",
    icon: "&#128274;",
    description: "CFTC/NFA oversight, fund segregation, 2FA, and ownership",
    questions: [
      { q: "Is Tradovate regulated?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate LLC is a registered Futures Commission Merchant (FCM) with the CFTC and a member of the NFA. You can look them up by name on NFA's BASIC database.</p>" },
      { q: "Are my Tradovate funds insured?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Customer futures funds are held in segregated accounts per CFTC Regulation 1.20, meaning Tradovate cannot commingle them with firm funds. SIPC does not cover futures accounts (SIPC is for securities), so segregation is the primary protection.</p>" },
      { q: "Does Tradovate have two-factor authentication?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — Tradovate supports 2FA by email or authenticator app on live accounts, and new-device logins require approval. Traders occasionally have to temporarily relax 2FA to connect a third-party integration that doesn't handle the approval email flow.</p>" },
      { q: "Who owns Tradovate?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>NinjaTrader acquired Tradovate in January 2022 for $115M, and NinjaTrader itself is a portfolio company of Long Ridge Equity Partners. Tradovate's founder Rick Tomsic joined NinjaTrader's executive team.</p>" },
      { q: "How does Tradovate protect against unauthorized access?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>2FA, device approval emails, encrypted sessions, and automatic session timeout cover the account layer. Always use a unique password and enable authenticator-app 2FA rather than SMS if you have a large funded account.</p>" },
      { q: "What happens to my money if Tradovate goes bankrupt?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Because futures funds are held in CFTC-segregated accounts, they would typically be transferred to another FCM rather than absorbed into the estate. History shows recovery is usually full but can take time — MF Global is the cautionary case worth reading about.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Prop Firms on Tradovate",
    icon: "&#127942;",
    description: "Which firms use Tradovate, automation compliance, and Topstep migration",
    questions: [
      { q: "Which prop firms use Tradovate?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Prop firms that clear through Tradovate include <strong>Apex Trader Funding</strong>, Take Profit Trader, My Funded Futures, Elite Trader Funding, TradeDay, Earn2Trade, Tradeify, Bulenox, FundedNext Futures, and several others. Topstep historically used Tradovate but migrated to its own TopstepX platform.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I automate a prop firm Tradovate account with PickMyTrade?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Yes — any prop firm that runs on Tradovate works with PickMyTrade, and PickMyTrade supports 9+ prop firm partners overall. Your prop firm's rules (daily loss, trailing drawdown, consistency) still apply exactly the same as manual trading.</p>" },
      { q: "Will automation get me in trouble with my prop firm?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Most Tradovate-clearing prop firms allow automation as long as you're not copying public signals or running HFT strategies that abuse their feed. Always read your specific prop firm's rules — some restrict news trading, outside session trading, or specific indicators.</p>" },
      { q: "Does Topstep still work with Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Topstep moved most of its trading to TopstepX, its own platform built on ProjectX, so new Topstep accounts primarily use TopstepX rather than Tradovate. If you need Tradovate specifically for automation, check current Topstep platform options before signing up.</p>" },
      { q: "Can I use PickMyTrade with Apex or other prop firms that run on Tradovate?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Yes — any prop firm that clears through Tradovate (Apex, TradeDay, Take Profit Trader, My Funded Futures, Elite Trader Funding, etc.) works with PickMyTrade the same way a personal Tradovate account does. You still have to respect the prop firm's trading rules — the automation does not bypass them.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I manage multiple prop firm accounts on Tradovate at once?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Tradovate supports multi-account trading from the same login, and automation tools like PickMyTrade can fire a single alert into 10+ linked accounts with individual size multipliers. The main gotcha is Tradovate's API rate limit when firing many orders in bursts.</p>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Troubleshooting & Errors",
    icon: "&#128295;",
    description: "Order rejections, disconnects, rate limits, and debug tips",
    questions: [
      { q: "Why is my Tradovate order being rejected?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Common rejection reasons are insufficient margin, exceeded max position / daily loss limit, 'No quote available' due to missing data subscription, the contract being in a restricted state (expiring, post-market), or prop firm risk rules blocking it. Tradovate's Orders module shows the exact rejection text per order.</p>" },
      { q: "What does 'No quote available' mean on Tradovate?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>It usually means your account doesn't have an active real-time market data subscription for that exchange, so the platform can't validate a market price. Subscribe to the CME bundle (or the specific exchange) and resubmit, or use a limit order at a known price.</p>" },
      { q: "Why does Tradovate keep logging me out?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Tradovate sessions time out after a period of inactivity, and 2FA on new devices forces re-approval. If you run a third-party integration and Tradovate doesn't see active user traffic, it will close the API session too.</p>" },
      { q: "My Tradovate connection keeps dropping — what should I do?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Check your local internet first (Tradovate is cloud, so your latency matters), then try a different browser or the desktop app, then check Tradovate's status page. If it's a Sunday-night data dry spell, Tradovate's server sometimes flags the session as stale even when you're connected.</p>" },
      { q: "Why am I getting '429 Too Many Requests' on Tradovate?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>You've exceeded the API rate limit — roughly 5,000 requests per rolling 60 minutes. Tradovate temporarily blocks new requests for 20–30 seconds, and repeated violations can upgrade to a 'P-ticket' penalty with a longer waiting period.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the API rate limit PickMyTrade has to respect on Tradovate?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Tradovate enforces approximately 5,000 requests per hour per session on a rolling 60-minute window, and they may return 429 Too Many Requests if you exceed it. PickMyTrade batches and throttles calls to stay under that limit, but firing 20+ rapid alerts back-to-back can still trigger a cooldown.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I debug a failed Tradovate automation trade?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Start in the PickMyTrade webhook log to confirm the alert arrived, then check the order status in Tradovate's Orders module for the rejection reason. 'Access is denied' usually means session expired, 'No quote available' usually means missing market data subscription, and 'Exceeded max position' is a prop firm risk rule.</p>" },
      { q: "What happens to my automation if Tradovate's API has an outage?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>If Tradovate's API is down, no webhook platform can place orders until it recovers — that's a broker-level issue, not an automation tool issue. PickMyTrade surfaces the API error in the alert log so you know whether the signal was received but rejected, versus never delivered.</p>" },
      { q: "What happens to my automated trades during the CME daily maintenance window (4:00–5:00 PM CT)?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The CME daily maintenance window runs from <strong>4:00 PM to 5:00 PM Central Time (CT)</strong> on weekdays, and during this period futures markets are closed. No new orders can be placed, and existing orders may behave differently depending on your broker and platform.</p><ul><li><strong>Markets are closed:</strong> All CME Group futures products (ES, NQ, CL, GC, etc.) halt trading. Any TradingView alert that fires during this window cannot result in an executed trade.</li><li><strong>Open orders may be cancelled:</strong> Some platforms cancel all resting (GTC, limit, stop) orders at 4:00 PM CT when the maintenance break starts. Others hold orders and resubmit them when markets reopen at 5:00 PM CT. Tradovate's behavior depends on the order type — check your specific order status after the break.</li><li><strong>Daily P&amp;L and margin reset:</strong> Your account's daily P&amp;L, margin calculations, and settlement prices update during this window. The 'new trading day' begins at 5:00 PM CT.</li><li><strong>Automation implications:</strong> If your TradingView strategy generates signals during the maintenance break, those alerts will fire but the corresponding orders will be queued or rejected depending on your setup. Ensure your strategy accounts for the daily close at 4:00 PM CT.</li></ul><p><strong>Weekend maintenance:</strong> CME markets close Friday at 4:00 PM CT and reopen Sunday at 5:00 PM CT. No trading or order submission is possible during the weekend. Plan your automated strategy accordingly to avoid holding positions over weekends unless intentional.</p>" },
      { q: "Why do ghost orders or phantom trades appear on Tradovate when using NinjaTrader, and how do I fix them?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Ghost orders</strong> are phantom positions that appear in your platform but don't exist at the broker level, or vice versa — real orders at the broker that your trading platform doesn't display. This mismatch is most common when using NinjaTrader connected to a Tradovate account and occurs due to connection-level sync failures.</p><p><strong>Common causes:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Connection drops during order confirmation:</strong> Your order is sent to Tradovate and filled, but the confirmation message never reaches NinjaTrader before the connection drops. NinjaTrader may show the order as unfilled or not display it at all, while Tradovate has an active position.</li><li><strong>OCO bracket desync:</strong> When placing OCO (bracket) orders through NinjaTrader on Tradovate, stop and target orders can become 'yellow' — unmovable and uncancellable from NinjaTrader. You must log into Tradovate directly to manage them.</li><li><strong>Stale cached position data:</strong> After a reconnect, NinjaTrader may display positions from a cached state that doesn't reflect the current broker reality.</li><li><strong>Trade copier conflicts:</strong> Running trade copiers like Replikanto alongside multiple Tradovate accounts can generate duplicate phantom positions, especially if ATM bracket templates are used.</li></ul><p><strong>How to fix and prevent ghost orders:</strong></p><ul><li>Always verify your positions in the Tradovate web/mobile platform — this shows the actual broker state.</li><li>After any connection interruption, flatten and reconcile before resuming trading.</li><li>Avoid using ATM bracket templates with trade copiers connected to Tradovate.</li><li>If using a webhook-based automation service like PickMyTrade instead of NinjaTrader, ghost orders are far less likely since the order flow goes directly to the Tradovate API without a desktop client intermediary.</li></ul>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    category: "Broker Comparisons",
    icon: "&#9878;",
    description: "Tradovate vs NinjaTrader, Rithmic, TradeStation, and ProjectX",
    questions: [
      { q: "Tradovate vs NinjaTrader — what's the difference?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Tradovate is fully cloud-based and owned by NinjaTrader since 2022, while NinjaTrader itself is a downloadable desktop platform with more advanced backtesting and a larger third-party indicator ecosystem. Casual traders and mobile users often prefer Tradovate; quants and platform power users often prefer NinjaTrader.</p>" },
      { q: "Tradovate vs Rithmic — which is better?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Rithmic is a low-latency execution and data infrastructure used by pro and algo traders, typically accessed via platforms like NinjaTrader or R|Trader Pro. Tradovate is a brokerage with its own cloud platform that's easier to start with but has shown higher tail-latency during major news events in independent slippage tests.</p>" },
      { q: "Tradovate vs TradeStation — which is better for futures?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>TradeStation is a full multi-asset brokerage (stocks, options, futures) with deep backtesting tools, while Tradovate is futures-only with simpler pricing and a more modern interface. If you want multi-asset and heavy strategy development, TradeStation; if you want pure futures with cloud access and cheap commissions, Tradovate.</p>" },
      { q: "Tradovate vs ProjectX — what's the difference?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>ProjectX is a newer cloud-native futures infrastructure that many prop firms (including TopstepX) migrated to in 2024–2025, often cited as competitive on speed and scalability. Tradovate has a longer track record, more third-party integrations, and a wider prop-firm footprint in 2026.</p>" },
      { q: "Is Tradovate better than NinjaTrader for automation?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>They're siblings under the same parent, but Tradovate's REST/WebSocket API is generally easier for third-party webhook automation tools to integrate with, while NinjaTrader favors its own NinjaScript/ATM strategies. For TradingView-webhook-based automation, Tradovate is typically the simpler target.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 10. TRADING FUNDAMENTALS ───
  {
    category: "Trading Fundamentals",
    icon: "&#128218;",
    color: "rgba(99,179,237,0.12)",
    description: "Core concepts every trader must know",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is trading and how does it differ from investing?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Trading</strong> involves buying and selling financial instruments (stocks, futures, options, currencies) over short time frames to profit from price fluctuations. <strong>Investing</strong> is buying assets to hold for the long term, typically years or decades, to build wealth through appreciation and dividends.</p><p>Key differences: traders focus on technical analysis and short-term price action; investors focus on fundamentals and long-term growth. Trading requires more active screen time, while investing is more passive.</p>" },
      { q: "What are the different types of financial markets?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The major financial markets include:</p><ul><li><strong>Stock Market</strong> - Buy/sell shares of publicly traded companies (NYSE, NASDAQ)</li><li><strong>Futures Market</strong> - Trade contracts for future delivery of commodities, indices, currencies (CME, CBOT)</li><li><strong>Options Market</strong> - Trade contracts giving the right to buy/sell at specific prices</li><li><strong>Forex Market</strong> - Trade currency pairs (EUR/USD, GBP/JPY); the largest market by daily volume ($7.5 trillion+)</li><li><strong>Cryptocurrency Market</strong> - Trade digital assets like Bitcoin, Ethereum on exchanges like Coinbase, Kraken</li><li><strong>Bond Market</strong> - Buy/sell government and corporate debt instruments</li><li><strong>Commodities Market</strong> - Trade physical goods like gold, oil, agricultural products</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is a stock and how do stock prices move?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>stock</strong> (or share) represents partial ownership in a company. When you buy stock in Apple, you own a tiny fraction of the company. Stock prices move based on <strong>supply and demand</strong>: when more people want to buy than sell, the price rises; when more want to sell than buy, it falls.</p><p>Price drivers include: company earnings reports, economic data, interest rate changes, investor sentiment, industry news, and global events. In the short term, prices are heavily influenced by emotion and speculation; in the long term, they tend to track a company's fundamental value.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between a bull market and a bear market?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>bull market</strong> is characterized by rising prices (typically 20%+ from recent lows), optimism, and strong economic conditions. Traders say the market is 'bullish' when they expect prices to go up.</p><p>A <strong>bear market</strong> is characterized by falling prices (typically 20%+ from recent highs), pessimism, and weakening economic conditions. Traders say the market is 'bearish' when they expect prices to fall.</p><div class='tip'>Historically, bull markets last much longer than bear markets. The average bull market lasts about 4.4 years, while the average bear market lasts about 11.3 months.</div>" },
      { q: "What are market hours and why do they matter?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>U.S. Stock Market:</strong> Regular trading hours are 9:30 AM - 4:00 PM ET. Pre-market runs 4:00 AM - 9:30 AM ET, and after-hours runs 4:00 PM - 8:00 PM ET.</p><p><strong>Futures Market:</strong> Nearly 23 hours/day, Sunday 6:00 PM - Friday 5:00 PM ET, with a 1-hour daily break.</p><p><strong>Forex Market:</strong> 24 hours/day, 5 days/week, across overlapping sessions (Sydney, Tokyo, London, New York).</p><p>Market hours matter because <strong>liquidity and volatility vary throughout the day</strong>. The first and last hours of regular stock trading typically see the highest volume. The London-New York overlap (8:00 AM - 12:00 PM ET) is the most active forex period.</p>" },
      { q: "What is liquidity and why is it important for traders?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Liquidity</strong> refers to how quickly and easily you can buy or sell an asset without significantly affecting its price. Highly liquid markets (like major stocks, EUR/USD) have tight bid-ask spreads and high volume, meaning you can enter and exit trades efficiently.</p><p><strong>Low liquidity</strong> means fewer buyers and sellers, wider spreads, and the risk of <strong>slippage</strong> (getting a worse price than expected). Penny stocks, exotic currency pairs, and thinly traded options are examples of low-liquidity instruments.</p><div class='tip'>As a general rule, always trade liquid instruments. It protects you from excessive slippage and ensures you can exit positions quickly when needed.</div>" },
      { q: "What is a pip, a tick, and a point in trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Pip</strong> (Percentage in Point): Used in forex. For most currency pairs, 1 pip = 0.0001 (the fourth decimal). For Japanese Yen pairs, 1 pip = 0.01. On a standard lot of EUR/USD, one pip is worth about $10.</p><p><strong>Tick</strong>: The minimum price increment a futures contract can move. For E-mini S&P 500 (ES), 1 tick = 0.25 points = $12.50. For Micro E-mini (MES), 1 tick = 0.25 points = $1.25.</p><p><strong>Point</strong>: A full-number price change. If ES moves from 5000.00 to 5001.00, that's 1 point = 4 ticks = $50 per contract.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the bid-ask spread and how does it affect my trades?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>bid price</strong> is the highest price a buyer is willing to pay. The <strong>ask price</strong> is the lowest price a seller is willing to accept. The <strong>spread</strong> is the difference between them.</p><p>For example, if a stock's bid is $50.00 and ask is $50.02, the spread is $0.02. When you buy at market, you pay the ask; when you sell, you receive the bid. This means every trade starts with a tiny built-in loss equal to the spread.</p><div class='warning'>Wide spreads eat into your profits, especially for frequent traders. Always check the spread before trading, particularly with options, penny stocks, and exotic instruments.</div>" },
      { q: "What is volatility and how is it measured?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Volatility</strong> measures how much and how fast prices fluctuate. High volatility means prices swing dramatically; low volatility means prices move in a narrow range.</p><p>Key volatility measures:</p><ul><li><strong>Historical Volatility (HV)</strong> - Based on past price movements over a specific period</li><li><strong>Implied Volatility (IV)</strong> - Derived from options prices; reflects market's expectation of future volatility</li><li><strong>VIX Index</strong> - The 'Fear Index'; measures expected 30-day volatility of the S&P 500</li><li><strong>ATR (Average True Range)</strong> - Shows the average range of price movement per period</li></ul><p>Traders use volatility to set stop losses, size positions, and choose strategies. Higher volatility means bigger potential profits AND bigger potential losses.</p>" },
      { q: "What is leverage and how does it work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Leverage</strong> lets you control a larger position with a smaller amount of capital. If you have 10:1 leverage, $1,000 controls $10,000 worth of assets.</p><p>Examples by market:</p><ul><li><strong>Futures:</strong> E-mini S&P 500 worth ~$250,000 requires only ~$12,000 margin (about 20:1 leverage)</li><li><strong>Forex:</strong> Retail brokers offer up to 50:1 leverage in the US</li><li><strong>Stocks:</strong> Margin accounts provide 2:1 leverage for day trading (up to 4:1 intraday for pattern day traders)</li></ul><div class='warning'>Leverage is a double-edged sword. It amplifies both profits AND losses. A 10% move against you with 10:1 leverage means a 100% loss of your margin. Many beginners blow up their accounts by using too much leverage.</div>" },
      { q: "What is margin in trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Margin</strong> is the amount of money you must deposit to open and maintain a leveraged position. It acts as a 'good faith deposit,' not a fee.</p><p><strong>Initial Margin:</strong> The minimum required to open a position. For futures, this is typically 2-12% of the contract's notional value.</p><p><strong>Maintenance Margin:</strong> The minimum equity you must maintain. If your account drops below this, you receive a <strong>margin call</strong> requiring you to add funds or your broker may liquidate your position.</p><p>Unlike stock margin (which is essentially a loan with interest), futures margin is a performance bond. You don't pay interest on futures margin, but you do on stock margin borrowing.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a margin call and how do I avoid one?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>margin call</strong> occurs when your account equity falls below the maintenance margin requirement. Your broker will notify you to deposit additional funds or reduce positions. If you don't act quickly, the broker may automatically liquidate positions at whatever price is available - often at a loss.</p><p>How to avoid margin calls:</p><ul><li>Never use more than 50% of available margin</li><li>Always use stop-loss orders</li><li>Size positions appropriately (risk only 1-2% per trade)</li><li>Monitor positions actively, especially during volatile periods</li><li>Keep extra cash in your account as a buffer</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is a broker and how do I choose one?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>broker</strong> is the intermediary that executes your trades on exchanges. They provide the trading platform, market data, and account management.</p><p><strong>Key factors when choosing a broker:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fees/commissions:</strong> Compare per-trade costs. Some offer commission-free stocks but charge for futures/options</li><li><strong>Platform quality:</strong> Charting tools, order types, speed, mobile app</li><li><strong>Asset classes:</strong> Does it offer what you want to trade (stocks, futures, options, forex, crypto)?</li><li><strong>Regulation:</strong> Ensure they're regulated (FINRA/SEC in the US, FCA in UK)</li><li><strong>Customer support:</strong> Test their responsiveness before committing real money</li><li><strong>Account minimums:</strong> Some require $0, others $2,000+</li></ul><p><strong>Popular brokers:</strong> Interactive Brokers (best overall), Tradovate (futures), TD Ameritrade/thinkorswim (options), Robinhood (beginners), NinjaTrader (futures).</p>" },
      { q: "What are the different trading styles (timeframes)?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Trading styles are defined by how long you hold positions:</p><ul><li><strong>Scalping:</strong> Seconds to minutes. Many trades per day. Tiny profits per trade.</li><li><strong>Day Trading:</strong> Minutes to hours. All positions closed by market close. No overnight risk.</li><li><strong>Swing Trading:</strong> Days to weeks. Captures larger moves. Less screen time needed.</li><li><strong>Position Trading:</strong> Weeks to months. Based on longer-term trends and fundamentals.</li><li><strong>Investing:</strong> Months to years. Buy and hold based on fundamental value.</li></ul><p>Shorter timeframes require more skill, more screen time, and more capital (for costs). Most beginners find success starting with swing trading - it allows time for analysis and doesn't require sitting at a screen all day.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a stock split and how does it affect my investment?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>stock split</strong> increases the number of shares while proportionally reducing the price per share. Your total investment value stays the same.</p><p><strong>Example (2:1 split):</strong> You own 10 shares at $200 ($2,000 total). After split, you own 20 shares at $100 ($2,000 total). Nothing changes except the number of shares and price per share.</p><p><strong>Reverse split:</strong> Combines shares to increase price. Often done by struggling companies to meet exchange listing requirements. Generally seen as a negative signal.</p><p>Splits can affect options contracts (strike prices and contract multipliers adjust), so check with your broker if you hold options during a split.</p>" },
      { q: "What is market capitalization and why does it matter?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Market cap</strong> = Share price x Total shares outstanding. It measures a company's total market value.</p><ul><li><strong>Mega cap:</strong> $200B+ (Apple, Microsoft, Nvidia)</li><li><strong>Large cap:</strong> $10B - $200B (established companies, lower risk)</li><li><strong>Mid cap:</strong> $2B - $10B (growth potential with moderate risk)</li><li><strong>Small cap:</strong> $300M - $2B (higher growth potential, higher volatility)</li><li><strong>Micro/Nano cap:</strong> Below $300M (speculative, very volatile, less liquid)</li></ul><p>Market cap affects volatility, liquidity, and institutional interest. Large-cap stocks are suitable for beginners; small-caps are riskier but can produce outsized returns.</p>" },
      { q: "What is inflation and how does it affect investments?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Inflation</strong> is the rate at which prices rise over time, eroding purchasing power. If inflation is 3%, your money buys 3% less next year.</p><p><strong>Impact on investments:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Stocks:</strong> Moderate inflation (2-3%) is generally fine for stocks. High inflation hurts because it raises costs and leads to higher interest rates</li><li><strong>Bonds:</strong> Inflation is the enemy of bonds. Rising inflation reduces the real value of fixed coupon payments</li><li><strong>Cash:</strong> Loses purchasing power every year. Keeping too much in cash is a hidden loss</li><li><strong>Real estate/Commodities:</strong> Generally benefit from inflation as hard assets appreciate</li><li><strong>Gold:</strong> Traditional inflation hedge, though not always correlated in the short term</li></ul><p>The CPI (Consumer Price Index) report is the most-watched inflation indicator and significantly moves markets.</p>" },
      { q: "What are bonds and how do interest rates affect them?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>bond</strong> is a loan you make to a government or corporation. They pay you fixed interest (coupon) and return your principal at maturity.</p><p><strong>Bond prices and interest rates have an inverse relationship:</strong> When rates rise, existing bond prices fall (because new bonds pay higher coupons, making old bonds less attractive). When rates fall, existing bond prices rise.</p><p><strong>Key terms:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Yield:</strong> The effective return on a bond (coupon / current price)</li><li><strong>Duration:</strong> Sensitivity to interest rate changes. Longer-duration bonds are more volatile</li><li><strong>Credit rating:</strong> AAA (safest) to junk/high-yield (riskiest). Higher risk = higher yield</li></ul><p>The 10-year Treasury yield is one of the most important numbers in finance. It influences mortgage rates, stock valuations, and currency markets.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 11. ORDER TYPES & EXECUTION ───
  {
    category: "Order Types & Execution",
    icon: "&#128203;",
    color: "rgba(79,209,197,0.12)",
    description: "How to place and manage your trades",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is a market order and when should I use it?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>market order</strong> executes immediately at the best available price. Use it when you need to enter or exit a trade quickly and the exact price is less important than guaranteed execution.</p><p><strong>Best for:</strong> Highly liquid instruments with tight spreads, emergency exits, fast-moving markets where you need to get in/out now.</p><div class='warning'>In illiquid markets or during high volatility, market orders can result in significant slippage (getting a much worse price than expected).</div>" },
      { q: "What is a limit order and how does it protect me?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>limit order</strong> specifies the maximum price you'll pay (buy limit) or minimum you'll accept (sell limit). It only executes at your price or better.</p><p><strong>Buy Limit:</strong> Set below current price - 'I want to buy, but only if it drops to $50 or lower.'</p><p><strong>Sell Limit:</strong> Set above current price - 'I want to sell, but only at $60 or higher.'</p><p>The trade-off: you get price control but no guarantee of execution. If the market never reaches your limit price, the order stays unfilled.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a stop-loss order and why is it critical?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>stop-loss order</strong> automatically exits your trade when price hits a specified level, limiting your potential loss. It's the most fundamental risk management tool.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> You buy stock at $100 and set a stop-loss at $95. If the price drops to $95, your position is automatically sold, capping your loss at $5/share (5%).</p><p><strong>Stop Market:</strong> Becomes a market order when triggered (guaranteed exit but possible slippage).</p><p><strong>Stop Limit:</strong> Becomes a limit order when triggered (price control but may not fill in a fast crash).</p><div class='tip'>Professional traders treat stop-losses as non-negotiable. Before entering ANY trade, know where your stop is. 'Hope is not a strategy.'</div>" },
      { q: "What is a trailing stop order?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>trailing stop</strong> automatically adjusts your stop-loss as the price moves in your favor. It 'trails' the price by a set amount (fixed dollar/percentage or ATR-based).</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> You buy at $100 with a $5 trailing stop. If price rises to $110, your stop moves to $105. If price then falls to $105, you're stopped out with a $5 profit instead of a potential $5 loss.</p><p>The trailing stop only moves in one direction (up for longs, down for shorts). It locks in profits while giving the trade room to run.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a bracket order (OCO)?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>bracket order</strong> places three orders simultaneously: your entry, a profit target (limit), and a stop-loss. When one exit order fills, the other automatically cancels.</p><p><strong>OCO (One-Cancels-Other)</strong> links two exit orders so only one executes. This automates your trade management completely.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Enter long ES at 5000. Set profit target at 5020 and stop-loss at 4990. If price hits 5020, you take profit and the stop cancels. If it hits 4990, you take the loss and the profit target cancels.</p><div class='tip'>Bracket orders are essential for automated traders and anyone who can't watch screens constantly. Most futures platforms (Tradovate, Rithmic, TradeStation) support them natively, and automation platforms like PickMyTrade can manage bracket orders automatically from your TradingView strategy.</div>" },
      { q: "What is slippage and how can I minimize it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Slippage</strong> is the difference between your expected execution price and the actual fill price. It commonly occurs during fast markets, low liquidity, or when using market orders.</p><p>Ways to reduce slippage:</p><ul><li>Trade liquid instruments with tight spreads</li><li>Use limit orders instead of market orders when possible</li><li>Avoid trading during major news releases if you can't handle slippage</li><li>Trade during peak market hours for maximum liquidity</li><li>Use realistic stop levels that won't trigger in normal noise</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between a GTC and a day order?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Day Order:</strong> Expires at the end of the current trading session if not filled. This is the default order type at most brokers.</p><p><strong>GTC (Good-Til-Cancelled):</strong> Remains active until filled or manually cancelled, potentially lasting weeks or months.</p><p>Other time-in-force options include <strong>IOC</strong> (Immediate or Cancel - fills what it can immediately, cancels the rest), <strong>FOK</strong> (Fill or Kill - must fill entirely immediately or cancel), and <strong>MOC</strong> (Market on Close - executes at the closing price).</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between paper trading and live trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Paper trading</strong> (simulated trading) uses virtual money to practice without financial risk. Most platforms offer this feature (TradingView's paper trading, NinjaTrader's sim, thinkorswim's paperMoney).</p><p>Key differences from live trading:</p><ul><li><strong>Execution:</strong> Paper trades fill instantly at displayed prices. Real orders face slippage and partial fills</li><li><strong>Emotions:</strong> No real money means no real fear or greed. This is the biggest gap</li><li><strong>Liquidity Impact:</strong> Paper trading ignores the effect your orders would have on the market</li></ul><div class='tip'>Paper trade for at least 2-3 months before going live. Track your performance as if it were real. When you're consistently profitable in sim, transition to live trading with small position sizes.</div>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 12. TECHNICAL ANALYSIS BASICS ───
  {
    category: "Technical Analysis",
    icon: "&#128200;",
    color: "rgba(246,173,85,0.12)",
    description: "Reading charts, indicators, and patterns",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is technical analysis and does it actually work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Technical analysis (TA)</strong> studies price charts and mathematical indicators to forecast future price movements. It's based on three principles: price discounts everything, price moves in trends, and history tends to repeat.</p><p>Does it work? TA is not a crystal ball, but it's a powerful framework for making probabilistic decisions. It works best when combined with proper risk management. No single indicator is reliable in isolation - professionals use confluence (multiple signals aligning).</p><p>Studies show that certain patterns and momentum indicators have statistical edges, but no method works 100% of the time. The key is consistent application with discipline.</p>" },
      { q: "What are support and resistance levels?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Support</strong> is a price level where buying pressure is strong enough to prevent the price from falling further. Think of it as a 'floor' where demand meets supply.</p><p><strong>Resistance</strong> is a price level where selling pressure prevents the price from rising further. Think of it as a 'ceiling.'</p><p>Key principles:</p><ul><li>The more times a level is tested, the stronger it becomes (but it weakens with each test)</li><li>Once broken, support becomes resistance and vice versa (role reversal)</li><li>Round numbers (e.g., $100, $5000) often act as psychological support/resistance</li><li>Volume at a level confirms its significance</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What are moving averages and which ones should I use?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>moving average (MA)</strong> smooths price data to show the trend direction. The two main types:</p><p><strong>Simple Moving Average (SMA):</strong> Arithmetic average of prices over N periods. Equal weight to all prices.</p><p><strong>Exponential Moving Average (EMA):</strong> Gives more weight to recent prices, reacting faster to changes.</p><p>Popular periods and uses:</p><ul><li><strong>9/10 EMA:</strong> Short-term momentum, scalping</li><li><strong>20/21 EMA:</strong> Short-term trend, swing entries</li><li><strong>50 SMA/EMA:</strong> Medium-term trend, institutional watch level</li><li><strong>200 SMA:</strong> Long-term trend, 'bull vs bear' dividing line</li></ul><p><strong>Golden Cross</strong> (50 MA crossing above 200 MA) is bullish. <strong>Death Cross</strong> (50 MA crossing below 200 MA) is bearish.</p>" },
      { q: "What is RSI and how do I read it?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>Relative Strength Index (RSI)</strong> is a momentum oscillator ranging from 0-100 that measures the speed and magnitude of recent price changes.</p><p><strong>Standard settings:</strong> 14-period RSI.</p><ul><li><strong>Above 70:</strong> Overbought (price may be due for a pullback)</li><li><strong>Below 30:</strong> Oversold (price may be due for a bounce)</li><li><strong>50 level:</strong> Acts as dynamic support/resistance in trending markets</li></ul><p><strong>RSI Divergence</strong> is a powerful signal: when price makes a new high but RSI makes a lower high, it suggests weakening momentum (bearish divergence). The opposite indicates bullish divergence.</p><div class='warning'>RSI can stay overbought/oversold for extended periods in strong trends. Don't blindly sell because RSI is above 70 in a bull market.</div>" },
      { q: "What is MACD and how do I use it?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>MACD (Moving Average Convergence Divergence)</strong> shows the relationship between two exponential moving averages. It consists of:</p><ul><li><strong>MACD Line:</strong> 12-period EMA minus 26-period EMA</li><li><strong>Signal Line:</strong> 9-period EMA of the MACD line</li><li><strong>Histogram:</strong> Visual difference between MACD and Signal lines</li></ul><p><strong>Key signals:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bullish crossover:</strong> MACD crosses above Signal line</li><li><strong>Bearish crossover:</strong> MACD crosses below Signal line</li><li><strong>Zero line cross:</strong> MACD crossing above/below zero indicates trend change</li><li><strong>Divergence:</strong> Price vs. MACD disagreement signals potential reversal</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What are Bollinger Bands and how do I trade with them?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Bollinger Bands</strong> consist of a 20-period SMA (middle band) with upper and lower bands set at 2 standard deviations away. They adapt to volatility: bands widen in volatile markets and squeeze in quiet markets.</p><p><strong>Trading strategies:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Bollinger Squeeze:</strong> When bands contract tightly, expect a big move (but direction is unknown)</li><li><strong>Band Touch:</strong> Price touching the upper band doesn't mean 'sell'; in trends, price rides the band</li><li><strong>Mean Reversion:</strong> In ranging markets, price tends to return to the middle band</li><li><strong>W-Bottoms/M-Tops:</strong> Double bottom/top patterns at the lower/upper bands</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the Stochastic Oscillator?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>Stochastic Oscillator</strong> compares a closing price to its price range over a given period (typically 14). It ranges from 0-100.</p><ul><li><strong>Above 80:</strong> Overbought zone</li><li><strong>Below 20:</strong> Oversold zone</li><li><strong>%K line:</strong> The fast stochastic line</li><li><strong>%D line:</strong> 3-period moving average of %K (the signal line)</li></ul><p><strong>Best uses:</strong> Works best in ranging/sideways markets. In strong trends, it can remain overbought/oversold for extended periods. Combine with trend analysis to filter signals - only take bullish stochastic signals in an uptrend and bearish signals in a downtrend.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the Average True Range (ATR) and how do I use it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>ATR</strong> measures volatility by calculating the average range of price movement over a period (typically 14). It doesn't indicate direction, only how much an asset typically moves.</p><p><strong>Practical uses:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Stop-loss placement:</strong> Set stops at 1.5-2x ATR below entry for longs. This accounts for normal price noise</li><li><strong>Position sizing:</strong> If ATR is high, reduce position size to keep dollar risk constant</li><li><strong>Profit targets:</strong> Use 2-3x ATR as a reasonable profit target</li><li><strong>Filter trades:</strong> If ATR is unusually low, expect a breakout. If unusually high, expect a pullback</li></ul><div class='tip'>ATR is one of the most practical indicators for day-to-day trade management. Learn it early.</div>" },
      { q: "What is the Ichimoku Cloud and how do I read it?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>The <strong>Ichimoku Kinko Hyo</strong> ('one-glance equilibrium chart') is a comprehensive indicator system that shows support/resistance, trend direction, and momentum all at once.</p><p><strong>Five components:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Tenkan-sen (Conversion Line):</strong> 9-period mid-point - short-term signal</li><li><strong>Kijun-sen (Base Line):</strong> 26-period mid-point - medium-term signal</li><li><strong>Senkou Span A:</strong> Average of Tenkan and Kijun, plotted 26 periods ahead (leading span)</li><li><strong>Senkou Span B:</strong> 52-period mid-point, plotted 26 periods ahead</li><li><strong>Chikou Span (Lagging Line):</strong> Current close plotted 26 periods back</li></ul><p>The <strong>Cloud (Kumo)</strong> between Span A and Span B acts as dynamic support/resistance. Green cloud = bullish, red cloud = bearish. Price above cloud = bullish trend; below = bearish.</p>" },
      { q: "What is Volume Profile and how is it different from regular volume?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Regular volume shows how much was traded in each time period. <strong>Volume Profile</strong> shows how much was traded at each <strong>price level</strong>, displayed as horizontal bars on your chart.</p><p><strong>Key components:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Point of Control (POC):</strong> The price with the most volume - represents 'fair value' and acts as a magnet</li><li><strong>Value Area (VA):</strong> Range containing ~70% of volume - where most trading occurred</li><li><strong>High Volume Nodes (HVN):</strong> Areas of high agreement (support/resistance)</li><li><strong>Low Volume Nodes (LVN):</strong> Areas price moved through quickly (breakout zones)</li></ul><p>Volume Profile reveals the 'true' support and resistance based on actual traded volume, not just price levels.</p>" },
      { q: "What is VWAP and why do institutional traders use it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>VWAP (Volume Weighted Average Price)</strong> is the average price an instrument has traded at throughout the day, weighted by volume. Institutions use it as a benchmark to measure execution quality.</p><ul><li><strong>Above VWAP:</strong> Buyers are in control (bullish bias)</li><li><strong>Below VWAP:</strong> Sellers are in control (bearish bias)</li><li><strong>At VWAP:</strong> Fair value zone, price often gravitates here</li></ul><p>VWAP is most useful for intraday trading. It resets each day. Institutional algorithms are programmed to buy below VWAP and sell above it, making it a self-fulfilling support/resistance level.</p>" },
      { q: "What are Fibonacci retracement levels and do they work?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Fibonacci retracements</strong> are horizontal lines drawn at key percentages derived from the Fibonacci sequence: 23.6%, 38.2%, 50%, 61.8%, and 78.6%.</p><p>After a significant price move, traders expect pullbacks to these levels before the trend resumes. Draw from swing low to swing high (uptrend) or swing high to swing low (downtrend).</p><p><strong>Key levels:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>38.2%:</strong> Shallow retracement in strong trends</li><li><strong>50%:</strong> Not technically Fibonacci but widely used</li><li><strong>61.8% (Golden Ratio):</strong> The most-watched level; strong support/resistance</li><li><strong>'Golden Pocket' (61.8%-65%):</strong> Optimal buy zone in pullbacks</li></ul><p>Fibonacci levels work because enough traders watch them to create self-fulfilling prophecy. They're most effective when they align with other support/resistance levels.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 13. CANDLESTICK PATTERNS ───
  {
    category: "Candlestick Patterns",
    icon: "&#127891;",
    color: "rgba(104,211,145,0.12)",
    description: "Reading price action through candle formations",
    questions: [
      { q: "How do I read a candlestick chart?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Each candlestick represents one time period (1 minute, 1 hour, 1 day, etc.) and shows four prices:</p><ul><li><strong>Open:</strong> The first price of the period</li><li><strong>Close:</strong> The last price of the period</li><li><strong>High:</strong> The highest price reached</li><li><strong>Low:</strong> The lowest price reached</li></ul><p>The <strong>body</strong> is the thick part between open and close. The <strong>wicks/shadows</strong> extend to the high and low. A green/white body means close > open (bullish). A red/black body means close < open (bearish).</p><p>Long bodies show strong momentum. Long wicks show rejection at that price level.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a Doji and what does it signal?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>Doji</strong> forms when the open and close are virtually identical, creating a cross-like shape. It signals <strong>indecision</strong> between buyers and sellers.</p><p><strong>Types of Doji:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard Doji:</strong> Equal shadows top and bottom - pure indecision</li><li><strong>Dragonfly Doji:</strong> Long lower shadow, no upper shadow - bullish after a downtrend</li><li><strong>Gravestone Doji:</strong> Long upper shadow, no lower shadow - bearish after an uptrend</li><li><strong>Long-legged Doji:</strong> Very long shadows both ways - extreme indecision</li></ul><p>A Doji after a strong trend often signals a potential reversal, but always wait for confirmation on the next candle.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a Hammer and Inverted Hammer pattern?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>Hammer</strong> forms at the bottom of a downtrend: small body at the top with a long lower shadow (at least 2x the body). It shows sellers pushed price down but buyers fought back to close near the high. This is a <strong>bullish reversal</strong> signal.</p><p>An <strong>Inverted Hammer</strong> also appears at the bottom of a downtrend: small body at the bottom with a long upper shadow. It shows buyers attempted a rally; if followed by a bullish candle, it confirms reversal.</p><p>The bearish equivalents: <strong>Hanging Man</strong> (looks like a hammer but at the top of an uptrend) and <strong>Shooting Star</strong> (like an inverted hammer at the top).</p>" },
      { q: "What are Engulfing patterns?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Bullish Engulfing:</strong> A small red candle followed by a larger green candle that completely engulfs the previous body. Found at the end of downtrends, it signals strong buying pressure is overpowering sellers. The larger the engulfing candle, the stronger the signal.</p><p><strong>Bearish Engulfing:</strong> A small green candle followed by a larger red candle that completely engulfs the previous body. Found at the end of uptrends, it signals sellers overwhelming buyers.</p><div class='tip'>Engulfing patterns are among the most reliable candlestick patterns, especially on higher timeframes (daily, weekly). They work about 60-70% of the time when confirmed by volume and other indicators.</div>" },
      { q: "What is a Morning Star and Evening Star pattern?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Morning Star</strong> (bullish reversal - 3 candles):</p><ul><li>Candle 1: Large red body (strong selling)</li><li>Candle 2: Small body that gaps down (indecision - doji makes it even stronger)</li><li>Candle 3: Large green body that closes into the first candle's body (strong buying)</li></ul><p><strong>Evening Star</strong> (bearish reversal - 3 candles):</p><ul><li>Candle 1: Large green body</li><li>Candle 2: Small body that gaps up</li><li>Candle 3: Large red body that closes into the first candle's body</li></ul><p>These are among the most powerful reversal patterns, especially on the daily chart with confirming volume.</p>" },
      { q: "What are the most reliable candlestick patterns for beginners?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Start with these high-probability patterns that appear frequently:</p><ul><li><strong>Engulfing Patterns</strong> - Easiest to spot, clear reversal signals</li><li><strong>Hammer/Shooting Star</strong> - Single candle reversals at key levels</li><li><strong>Doji at extremes</strong> - Indecision at support/resistance = potential reversal</li><li><strong>Three White Soldiers / Three Black Crows</strong> - Three consecutive strong candles signal trend continuation</li><li><strong>Inside Bar</strong> - Small candle within previous candle's range = consolidation before breakout</li></ul><p><strong>Crucial rule:</strong> Never trade a candlestick pattern in isolation. Always confirm with: (1) where it forms (support/resistance), (2) volume, and (3) the broader trend context.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a pin bar and how do I trade it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>pin bar</strong> (pinocchio bar) has a long wick/shadow on one side and a small body on the other, showing price rejection at a level.</p><p><strong>Bullish pin bar:</strong> Long lower wick, small body near the top. Price went down but was rejected strongly. Bullish signal when found at support.</p><p><strong>Bearish pin bar:</strong> Long upper wick, small body near the bottom. Price went up but was rejected. Bearish signal when found at resistance.</p><p><strong>How to trade:</strong></p><ol><li>Identify pin bar at a key level (support, resistance, moving average, Fibonacci)</li><li>Enter on the break of the pin bar's body (above for bullish, below for bearish)</li><li>Stop-loss beyond the wick of the pin bar</li><li>Target at least 2x the risk (next S/R level)</li></ol>" },
      { q: "What is an inside bar pattern?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>An <strong>inside bar</strong> is a candle whose entire range (high to low) fits within the range of the previous candle. It signals consolidation and potential breakout.</p><p><strong>Trading inside bars:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Breakout approach:</strong> Place a buy stop above the mother bar's high and a sell stop below its low. Trade whichever direction breaks first</li><li><strong>With-trend approach:</strong> Only trade inside bar breakouts in the direction of the prevailing trend</li><li><strong>Multiple inside bars:</strong> Several inside bars in a row = tight compression = expect a powerful breakout</li></ul><p>Inside bars are particularly powerful on the daily chart. On lower timeframes, they're less reliable due to market noise.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a double top and double bottom pattern?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Double Top (bearish reversal):</strong> Price reaches a high, pulls back, rallies back to approximately the same high, then falls. Creates an 'M' shape. Confirms when price breaks below the pullback low (neckline).</p><p><strong>Double Bottom (bullish reversal):</strong> Price reaches a low, bounces, drops back to approximately the same low, then rises. Creates a 'W' shape. Confirms when price breaks above the bounce high (neckline).</p><p><strong>Trading:</strong> Enter on the neckline break. Stop-loss above the double top or below the double bottom. Target = distance from pattern high to neckline, projected from the breakout point.</p><p>These are among the most reliable chart patterns because they show clear failure to continue a trend.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a head and shoulders pattern?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>head and shoulders</strong> is a reversal pattern with three peaks: left shoulder, head (highest), and right shoulder. A 'neckline' connects the lows between the shoulders.</p><p><strong>Trading the pattern:</strong></p><ol><li>Identify the three peaks with the head being the highest</li><li>Draw the neckline connecting the two troughs</li><li>Enter short when price breaks below the neckline</li><li>Stop-loss above the right shoulder</li><li>Target = head-to-neckline distance projected downward from breakout</li></ol><p><strong>Inverse head and shoulders:</strong> Same pattern but flipped - three troughs with the head being the lowest. This is a bullish reversal signal at the bottom of a downtrend.</p><p>Volume typically decreases from left shoulder to head to right shoulder, confirming weakening momentum.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 14. RISK MANAGEMENT ───
  {
    category: "Risk Management",
    icon: "&#128737;",
    color: "rgba(252,129,129,0.12)",
    description: "Protecting your capital like a professional",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is the 1-2% rule in trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>1-2% rule</strong> states you should never risk more than 1-2% of your total trading capital on any single trade. This protects you from catastrophic drawdowns during losing streaks.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> With a $10,000 account risking 1% per trade, your maximum loss per trade is $100. Even 10 consecutive losing trades only costs 10% of your account.</p><p>Why it works mathematically: if you lose 50% of your account, you need a 100% gain just to get back to even. Keeping losses small prevents you from digging holes you can't climb out of.</p><div class='tip'>Most professional traders risk 0.5-1% per trade. Beginners should start at 0.5% until they have a proven edge.</div>" },
      { q: "How do I calculate position size?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Position Size Formula:</strong></p><p><code>Position Size = (Account Risk $) / (Distance to Stop Loss)</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> $25,000 account, risk 1% = $250 risk. Entry at $50, stop-loss at $48 = $2 distance. Position size = $250 / $2 = 125 shares.</p><p>For futures: $25,000 account, risk 1% = $250. ES stop-loss at 5 points = $250 per contract. Position size = $250 / $250 = 1 contract.</p><p>This ensures consistent risk regardless of the instrument or volatility. Every trade risks the same dollar amount.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the risk-reward ratio and what ratio should I target?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>risk-reward ratio (R:R)</strong> compares potential loss to potential profit. If you risk $100 to make $300, your R:R is 1:3.</p><p><strong>Calculation:</strong> R:R = (Entry - Stop Loss) / (Target - Entry)</p><p><strong>Minimum recommended:</strong> 1:2 or higher. This means you can be wrong 60% of the time and still be profitable.</p><p>Win rate and R:R relationship:</p><ul><li>1:1 R:R = Need >50% win rate to profit</li><li>1:2 R:R = Need >33% win rate to profit</li><li>1:3 R:R = Need >25% win rate to profit</li></ul><p>Always calculate R:R before entering a trade. If the math doesn't work, skip the trade.</p>" },
      { q: "What is maximum drawdown and why should I track it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Maximum drawdown</strong> is the largest peak-to-trough decline in your account value. If your account grows to $15,000 then drops to $12,000, your max drawdown is $3,000 (20%).</p><p>Why it matters:</p><ul><li>Shows the worst-case scenario of your strategy</li><li>Helps you set realistic expectations</li><li>Determines if your strategy is suitable for your risk tolerance</li><li>Psychologically, most traders abandon strategies after 20-30% drawdown</li></ul><p>Recovery from drawdown is exponentially harder: 10% loss requires 11% gain; 20% requires 25%; 50% requires 100%. This is why keeping drawdowns small is critical.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I diversify my trading portfolio?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Diversification in trading</strong> means spreading risk across uncorrelated assets and strategies:</p><ul><li><strong>Asset diversification:</strong> Trade different instruments (stocks, futures, forex) that don't move in lockstep</li><li><strong>Strategy diversification:</strong> Use multiple strategies (trend following, mean reversion, breakout)</li><li><strong>Timeframe diversification:</strong> Combine positions across different timeframes (day trades + swing trades)</li><li><strong>Sector diversification:</strong> Don't have all positions in one sector (e.g., all tech stocks)</li></ul><div class='warning'>Correlation increases during market stress. Assets that normally move independently can all drop together in a crash. Don't assume diversification protects you in extreme events.</div>" },
      { q: "What is a trailing stop and when should I use one?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>trailing stop</strong> automatically moves your stop-loss in the direction of profit, locking in gains as the trade runs.</p><p><strong>Methods:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fixed dollar/points:</strong> Trail by $2, 10 ticks, etc.</li><li><strong>ATR-based:</strong> Trail by 2x ATR (adapts to volatility)</li><li><strong>Structure-based:</strong> Move stop below each new higher low (uptrend)</li><li><strong>Moving average:</strong> Use the 20 EMA as your trailing stop reference</li></ul><p><strong>Best for:</strong> Trending markets. Avoid trailing stops in choppy, range-bound markets as you'll get stopped out by noise.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between risk management and money management?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Risk management</strong> deals with protecting individual trades: stop-losses, position sizing, risk-reward ratios, and managing open positions.</p><p><strong>Money management</strong> is the bigger picture: how much total capital to allocate to trading, when to increase/decrease position sizes based on equity, when to stop trading after consecutive losses, and how to handle profits (withdraw vs. compound).</p><p>Both are essential. Good risk management without money management leads to slow bleeding. Good money management without risk management leads to sudden account destruction.</p>" },
      { q: "How many trades should I have open at once?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>This depends on your account size, strategy, and ability to manage multiple positions. General guidelines:</p><ul><li><strong>Day traders:</strong> 1-3 positions at a time (focus and speed required)</li><li><strong>Swing traders:</strong> 5-8 positions (diversified but manageable)</li><li><strong>Position traders:</strong> 10-15 positions (longer holds, less active management)</li></ul><p><strong>Key rule:</strong> Total portfolio risk should never exceed 5-6% at any time. If you risk 1% per trade, that means a maximum of 5-6 simultaneous positions.</p><p>Watch for correlation between open positions. Five 'different' tech stocks is essentially one big bet on tech.</p>" },
      { q: "Should I use a fixed stop-loss or a mental stop?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Always use a fixed, placed stop-loss.</strong> Mental stops rely on you having the discipline to execute when emotions are screaming at you to hold.</p><p>Studies show that traders consistently violate their mental stops. The pain of taking a loss causes 'paralysis' - you tell yourself 'it'll come back' and the loss grows. A hard stop removes the decision from the equation.</p><p>The only exception is experienced scalpers in extremely liquid markets who actively manage every tick, but even they have a 'disaster stop' placed at a maximum loss level.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the Kelly Criterion for position sizing?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>The <strong>Kelly Criterion</strong> is a mathematical formula for optimal bet sizing: <code>K% = W - [(1-W)/R]</code> where W = win rate and R = win/loss ratio.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> 55% win rate, average win $200, average loss $100 (R=2). Kelly = 0.55 - [(1-0.55)/2] = 0.55 - 0.225 = 0.325 = 32.5% of account per trade.</p><p><strong>In practice:</strong> Full Kelly is extremely aggressive and leads to large drawdowns. Most traders use <strong>fractional Kelly</strong> (1/4 to 1/2 Kelly) for smoother equity curves. With the above example, half-Kelly = 16.25% per trade.</p><div class='warning'>Kelly requires accurate win rate and R:R data. Using inaccurate stats leads to over-sizing. Only apply Kelly after tracking at least 100+ trades.</div>" },
      { q: "What is correlation risk and how do I manage it?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Correlation risk</strong> occurs when multiple positions in your portfolio move in the same direction simultaneously, amplifying losses.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> You're long AAPL, MSFT, GOOGL, NVDA, and META. You think you have 5 separate positions, but they're all Big Tech and highly correlated. If tech sells off, ALL five positions lose simultaneously. Your real risk is 5x what you think.</p><p><strong>How to manage:</strong></p><ul><li>Check correlation between your positions using a correlation matrix</li><li>Treat highly correlated positions as one combined position for risk calculation</li><li>Diversify across uncorrelated sectors and asset classes</li><li>Be especially cautious during market stress, when correlations spike toward 1.0</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What are black swan events and how do I protect against them?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>A <strong>black swan</strong> is an extremely rare, unpredictable event with massive impact. Examples: 2008 financial crisis, COVID crash (March 2020), Swiss Franc de-pegging (Jan 2015), Flash Crash (May 2010).</p><p><strong>Protection strategies:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Always use stops:</strong> Even if you think 'it can't happen,' it can</li><li><strong>Avoid massive leverage:</strong> The Swiss Franc move wiped out accounts in seconds because brokers couldn't execute stops fast enough</li><li><strong>Tail-risk hedging:</strong> Buy far OTM puts as portfolio insurance (cheap but pays off massively in crashes)</li><li><strong>Position sizing:</strong> No single trade should risk more than 1-2%. Even a black swan can't destroy you</li><li><strong>Cash reserves:</strong> Keep some powder dry. Black swans create the best buying opportunities</li></ul>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 15. TRADING PSYCHOLOGY ───
  {
    category: "Trading Psychology",
    icon: "&#129504;",
    color: "rgba(183,148,244,0.12)",
    description: "Mastering the mental game of trading",
    questions: [
      { q: "Why do most traders lose money?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Statistics show that <strong>70-90% of retail traders lose money</strong>. The primary reasons are psychological, not technical:</p><ul><li><strong>No edge:</strong> Trading without a tested, proven strategy</li><li><strong>Poor risk management:</strong> Risking too much per trade, no stop-losses</li><li><strong>Overtrading:</strong> Trading out of boredom, revenge, or FOMO</li><li><strong>Emotional decisions:</strong> Letting fear and greed override the plan</li><li><strong>Unrealistic expectations:</strong> Expecting to double an account monthly</li><li><strong>No trading journal:</strong> Not tracking and learning from mistakes</li><li><strong>Undercapitalization:</strong> Not enough capital to survive the learning curve</li></ul>" },
      { q: "How do I control fear and greed when trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Fear</strong> causes traders to exit winners too early, avoid trades, and freeze during losses. <strong>Greed</strong> causes traders to overtrade, increase position sizes recklessly, and hold losers hoping for recovery.</p><p><strong>Practical techniques:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pre-define everything:</strong> Entry, stop, target - BEFORE the trade. Execute the plan, not the emotion</li><li><strong>Risk small:</strong> If a trade can meaningfully hurt you, the position is too large</li><li><strong>Use bracket orders:</strong> Automate exits so emotions can't interfere. Tools like PickMyTrade can execute your TradingView strategy automatically, removing human emotion entirely</li><li><strong>Take breaks:</strong> After 2-3 consecutive losses, stop trading for the day</li><li><strong>Journal emotions:</strong> Track how you felt during each trade to identify patterns</li><li><strong>Meditate/exercise:</strong> Pre-session routines that center your mindset</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is revenge trading and how do I stop it?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Revenge trading</strong> is taking impulsive trades after a loss, trying to 'win it back.' It's driven by frustration and ego, not analysis. It's one of the fastest ways to blow up an account.</p><p><strong>Warning signs:</strong> Trading larger than normal, abandoning your setup rules, trading immediately after a loss without pausing, feeling angry at the market.</p><p><strong>How to stop:</strong></p><ul><li>Set a <strong>daily loss limit</strong> (e.g., -$300 or -2%) and stop when you hit it</li><li>After any losing trade, take a 15-minute break minimum</li><li>After 3 consecutive losses, stop trading for the session</li><li>Review the losing trade objectively before the next trade</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is FOMO in trading and how do I overcome it?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>FOMO (Fear Of Missing Out)</strong> drives traders to chase prices, enter late, buy at tops, and abandon their trading plans. Social media amplifies FOMO when you see others posting winning trades.</p><p><strong>Antidotes to FOMO:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Accept that you'll miss trades.</strong> There are unlimited opportunities; no need to catch them all</li><li><strong>Stick to your setups.</strong> Only trade when YOUR criteria are met, not because the market is moving</li><li><strong>Remember:</strong> The best trades often set up during boring, quiet markets - not during FOMO spikes</li><li><strong>Mute financial social media</strong> during trading hours</li></ul><div class='tip'>A missed trade costs you $0. A bad trade driven by FOMO can cost you hundreds or thousands. The math is simple.</div>" },
      { q: "Why is a trading journal important and what should I track?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>trading journal</strong> is the single most powerful tool for improvement. It turns emotions into data and reveals patterns you'd never see otherwise.</p><p><strong>What to track:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trade details:</strong> Date, instrument, direction, entry/exit prices, size</li><li><strong>Setup name:</strong> What pattern/signal triggered the trade</li><li><strong>Risk/reward:</strong> Planned vs. actual R:R</li><li><strong>Outcome:</strong> P/L in dollars and R-multiples</li><li><strong>Screenshot:</strong> Chart at entry and exit</li><li><strong>Emotional state:</strong> How you felt before, during, after</li><li><strong>Grade:</strong> A (followed plan), B (minor deviation), C (broke rules)</li><li><strong>Lessons:</strong> What would you do differently?</li></ul><p>Review your journal weekly. Look for patterns in your best and worst trades.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I build discipline as a trader?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Discipline</strong> is the bridge between your trading plan and your trading results. Without it, even the best strategy fails.</p><p><strong>Building discipline:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trade small until it's boring.</strong> Reduce position size until you feel nothing about wins or losses. Then slowly increase</li><li><strong>Create and follow a daily routine:</strong> Pre-market preparation, trading hours, post-market review</li><li><strong>Write rules and tape them to your monitor.</strong> When tempted to break them, read them aloud</li><li><strong>Use a trading checklist.</strong> Before every trade, go through 5-7 criteria. If any fail, no trade</li><li><strong>Set daily limits:</strong> Maximum trades, maximum losses, stop time</li><li><strong>Reward process, not outcomes.</strong> An A+ trade that loses money is better than a sloppy trade that profits</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is tilt in trading and how do I recognize it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Tilt</strong> (borrowed from poker) is an emotional state where frustration, anger, or overconfidence impairs your decision-making. You start making irrational trades.</p><p><strong>Signs you're on tilt:</strong></p><ul><li>Taking trades that don't match your plan</li><li>Increasing position size after losses</li><li>Feeling personally attacked by the market</li><li>Saying 'just one more trade'</li><li>Physical symptoms: elevated heart rate, shallow breathing, clenched jaw</li></ul><p><strong>What to do:</strong> Stop trading immediately. Walk away. Exercise. Come back tomorrow. No trade is worth making while on tilt. The market will be there tomorrow; your capital might not be.</p>" },
      { q: "How long does it take to become a profitable trader?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Most sources agree on <strong>1-3 years of consistent effort</strong> to reach consistent profitability. Some faster, many never get there.</p><p><strong>Realistic timeline:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Months 1-6:</strong> Learning fundamentals, paper trading, developing a strategy</li><li><strong>Months 6-12:</strong> Small live account, experiencing real emotions, making mistakes</li><li><strong>Year 1-2:</strong> Refining strategy, building consistency, managing psychology</li><li><strong>Year 2-3:</strong> Increasing size, developing confidence, potential profitability</li></ul><p>The traders who succeed treat it like a profession: they study daily, journal every trade, and accept that the learning curve has a tuition cost.</p>" },
      { q: "What is confirmation bias and how does it hurt traders?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Confirmation bias</strong> is the tendency to seek information that supports your existing belief while ignoring contradicting evidence. In trading, this means you see only the bullish signals when you're already long, and only bearish signals when you're short.</p><p><strong>Examples in trading:</strong></p><ul><li>Watching only bullish YouTube videos about a stock you already own</li><li>Ignoring bearish indicators because you're committed to a bullish thesis</li><li>Reading only positive earnings analysis while dismissing cautious analysts</li></ul><p><strong>How to combat it:</strong> Before every trade, actively look for reasons NOT to take it. Write down 3 reasons the trade could fail. Follow traders/analysts who disagree with your view. If you can't find a strong counter-argument, the trade is probably better.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the sunk cost fallacy in trading?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>sunk cost fallacy</strong> is holding a losing position because of the money/time already invested, rather than making a rational decision about the future.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> You bought a stock at $100. It drops to $60. Instead of cutting losses, you think 'I've already lost $40/share, I can't sell now' and hold. The stock drops to $30.</p><p>The money you've already lost is <strong>gone regardless of your decision</strong>. The only question is: 'If I didn't own this right now, would I buy it at this price?' If the answer is no, sell. Past losses are irrelevant to future decisions.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I handle a losing streak?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Losing streaks are inevitable, even for profitable traders. A strategy with a 60% win rate has a ~1.3% chance of 7 consecutive losses. It WILL happen eventually.</p><p><strong>What to do:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reduce position size</strong> by 50% after 3 consecutive losses</li><li><strong>Review your journal.</strong> Are you following your plan? If yes, it's just variance. If no, fix the discipline issue</li><li><strong>Take a break</strong> if emotions are taking over (1-3 days away from screens)</li><li><strong>Check market conditions.</strong> Your strategy may not suit the current environment (e.g., a trend strategy in a choppy market)</li><li><strong>Never chase losses.</strong> Accept that recovering slowly is better than making it worse quickly</li></ul><p>The professionals know that losing streaks are statistical certainties. They survive by keeping losses small and their head clear.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 16. VIX & VOLATILITY TRADING ───
  {
    category: "VIX & Volatility Trading",
    icon: "&#9889;",
    color: "rgba(127,156,245,0.12)",
    description: "Using the Fear Index and volatility as a tool",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is the VIX and why is it called the 'Fear Index'?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>VIX (CBOE Volatility Index)</strong> measures the market's expectation of 30-day volatility for the S&P 500, derived from options prices. It's called the 'Fear Index' because it spikes when investors are scared and buy protective options.</p><ul><li><strong>VIX below 15:</strong> Low fear, complacent market, calm conditions</li><li><strong>VIX 15-20:</strong> Normal market conditions</li><li><strong>VIX 20-30:</strong> Elevated concern, increased hedging</li><li><strong>VIX above 30:</strong> High fear, often during sell-offs or crises</li><li><strong>VIX above 40:</strong> Panic (2008 crisis hit 80+, COVID crash hit 82)</li></ul>" },
      { q: "How do I use the VIX in my trading decisions?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The VIX isn't just for volatility traders - it provides valuable context for ALL traders:</p><ul><li><strong>Contrarian indicator:</strong> Extremely high VIX (above 30-35) often marks market bottoms. Extreme fear = buying opportunity (but use confirmation, don't catch falling knives)</li><li><strong>Very low VIX (below 12):</strong> Complacency. The next spike can cause sharp sell-offs. Tighten stops on longs</li><li><strong>Rising VIX + falling market:</strong> Confirms bearish momentum. Don't buy the dip yet</li><li><strong>Falling VIX + rising market:</strong> Confirms bullish momentum. Stay long</li><li><strong>VIX divergence:</strong> Market making new highs but VIX rising? Warning sign - smart money is buying protection</li></ul>" },
      { q: "Can I trade the VIX directly?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>You <strong>cannot</strong> trade the VIX index directly. Instead, you trade VIX derivatives:</p><ul><li><strong>VIX Futures:</strong> Trade on CBOE Futures Exchange. Nearly 23 hours/day. Settlement based on VIX Special Opening Quotation</li><li><strong>VIX Options:</strong> European-style (exercise only at expiration). Very popular for hedging</li><li><strong>VIX ETFs/ETNs:</strong> VXX, UVXY (long volatility), SVXY (short volatility)</li></ul><div class='warning'><strong>Critical warning about VIX ETFs:</strong> Due to contango (futures price > spot price), long VIX ETFs lose 8-13% per month on average through roll decay. They are NOT buy-and-hold instruments. They're designed for short-term hedging only.</div>" },
      { q: "What is implied volatility and how does it affect options prices?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Implied Volatility (IV)</strong> represents the market's forecast of how much an underlying asset is likely to move. It's a key factor in options pricing.</p><ul><li><strong>High IV:</strong> Options are expensive (market expects big moves). Favor selling options strategies (credit spreads, iron condors)</li><li><strong>Low IV:</strong> Options are cheap (market expects small moves). Favor buying options strategies (long calls/puts, debit spreads)</li></ul><p><strong>IV Rank/Percentile</strong> tells you if current IV is high or low relative to its own history. An IV percentile of 80% means current IV is higher than 80% of all readings in the past year.</p><p>After events (earnings, FOMC), IV typically 'crushes' - drops sharply. This is why options buyers often lose even when they get the direction right.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the VIX term structure and why does it matter?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>The <strong>VIX term structure</strong> shows VIX futures prices across different expiration dates. It's normally in <strong>contango</strong> (longer-dated futures priced higher than shorter-dated).</p><ul><li><strong>Contango (normal):</strong> Market is calm, no immediate fear. Long VIX ETFs bleed value from roll cost</li><li><strong>Backwardation (inverted):</strong> Near-term fear exceeds long-term. This happens during market crashes and signals extreme stress. Often a contrarian buy signal for stocks</li></ul><p>Monitoring the term structure helps you time VIX trades. Trading long volatility in contango is expensive; trading it in backwardation gives you roll yield working in your favor.</p>" },
      { q: "How do professional traders use volatility for position sizing?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>Professionals use <strong>volatility-adjusted position sizing</strong> to maintain consistent risk across different market environments:</p><p><strong>Method:</strong> Divide your standard risk amount by the current ATR (or IV) to determine position size. When volatility doubles, your position halves automatically.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Normally risk $500 per trade. ATR is usually 10 points. Today ATR is 20 points. Position size = ($500 / 20) = 25 shares instead of the usual ($500 / 10) = 50 shares.</p><p>This ensures a volatile day doesn't cause outsized losses. The Kelly Criterion and fixed fractional methods are more sophisticated versions of this concept used by quant funds. When automating through PickMyTrade, you can configure position sizing rules that automatically adapt to your risk parameters.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 17. OPEN INTEREST & VOLUME ANALYSIS ───
  {
    category: "Open Interest & Volume",
    icon: "&#128202;",
    color: "rgba(246,173,85,0.12)",
    description: "Reading market participation and conviction",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is open interest and how is it different from volume?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Volume</strong> counts every contract traded in a period (including opening and closing trades). <strong>Open interest (OI)</strong> counts only the active, open contracts at end of day.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Trader A buys 1 contract from Trader B. Volume = 1, OI increases by 1 (new position created). If Trader A then sells that contract to Trader C, Volume = 2, but OI stays the same (position transferred, not closed). If Trader A sells to close to Trader B who also closes, Volume = 1 again, but OI decreases by 1 (position eliminated).</p>" },
      { q: "How do I use open interest to confirm trends?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Open interest changes alongside price tell you about the <strong>strength of the trend</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Rising price + Rising OI:</strong> New money entering long side. Strong bullish trend confirmation</li><li><strong>Rising price + Falling OI:</strong> Short covering (shorts closing). Weak rally, may not last</li><li><strong>Falling price + Rising OI:</strong> New money entering short side. Strong bearish trend confirmation</li><li><strong>Falling price + Falling OI:</strong> Long liquidation (longs closing). Weak decline, selling may exhaust soon</li></ul><div class='tip'>OI analysis is particularly powerful in futures and options markets. Combine it with volume analysis for the clearest picture of market conviction.</div>" },
      { q: "What is the put/call ratio and how do I read it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>put/call ratio</strong> divides the number of traded put options by the number of traded call options. It's a sentiment indicator.</p><ul><li><strong>Ratio > 1.0:</strong> More puts than calls being traded - bearish sentiment, fear is dominant</li><li><strong>Ratio < 0.7:</strong> More calls than puts - bullish sentiment, greed/complacency is dominant</li><li><strong>Extreme readings:</strong> Used as a contrarian indicator. Extreme put buying (ratio > 1.2) often marks market bottoms. Extreme call buying (ratio < 0.5) often marks tops</li></ul><p>Look at the <strong>equity-only</strong> put/call ratio for the best sentiment reading, as the total ratio includes index hedging that skews the data.</p>" },
      { q: "What does high volume on a candle mean?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Volume is the <strong>fuel that drives price</strong>. High volume confirms price moves; low volume questions them.</p><ul><li><strong>High volume + big green candle:</strong> Strong buying conviction. Trend likely to continue up</li><li><strong>High volume + big red candle:</strong> Strong selling conviction. Trend likely to continue down</li><li><strong>High volume + small body (doji):</strong> Intense battle between buyers and sellers. Potential reversal</li><li><strong>High volume at support/resistance:</strong> That level is significant. Watch for breakout or bounce</li><li><strong>Breakout on high volume:</strong> Valid breakout. Likely to follow through</li><li><strong>Breakout on low volume:</strong> Suspect. May be a false breakout/trap</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the options max pain theory?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Max Pain</strong> is the price at which the total value of all outstanding options (puts and calls) at expiration would cause the greatest financial loss to option holders. The theory suggests that price gravitates toward max pain by expiration because options sellers (market makers) hedge their positions in ways that push price toward this level.</p><p><strong>How to use it:</strong></p><ul><li>Check max pain for weekly/monthly expirations</li><li>If price is far from max pain with expiration approaching, expect a gravitational pull</li><li>Most useful for index options (SPY, QQQ) in the final 2-3 days before expiration</li></ul><p>It's a guide, not a guarantee. Works better in calm markets than during high-event volatility.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 18. OPTIONS TRADING ───
  {
    category: "Options Trading",
    icon: "&#9878;",
    color: "rgba(79,209,197,0.12)",
    description: "Calls, puts, Greeks, and options strategies",
    questions: [
      { q: "What are options and how do they work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>An <strong>option</strong> is a contract giving the buyer the right (not obligation) to buy or sell an underlying asset at a specific price (strike) before a specific date (expiration).</p><ul><li><strong>Call Option:</strong> Right to BUY. You buy calls if you're bullish (expect price to rise)</li><li><strong>Put Option:</strong> Right to SELL. You buy puts if you're bearish (expect price to fall)</li></ul><p>Each options contract represents 100 shares. A call priced at $2.50 costs $250 (100 x $2.50).</p><p><strong>Buyer:</strong> Pays premium, limited risk (premium paid), unlimited profit potential on calls.</p><p><strong>Seller (Writer):</strong> Receives premium, unlimited risk on naked calls, profits from time decay.</p>" },
      { q: "What are the Options Greeks (Delta, Gamma, Theta, Vega)?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>Greeks</strong> measure how sensitive an option's price is to various factors:</p><p><strong>Delta (Direction):</strong> How much the option price moves per $1 move in the stock. Call delta: 0 to 1. Put delta: -1 to 0. At-the-money options have ~0.50 delta.</p><p><strong>Gamma (Acceleration):</strong> How fast delta changes. Highest for at-the-money options near expiration. Think of it as delta's 'gas pedal.'</p><p><strong>Theta (Time Decay):</strong> How much value the option loses per day. Enemy of option buyers, friend of option sellers. Accelerates as expiration approaches.</p><p><strong>Vega (Volatility):</strong> How much the option price changes per 1% change in implied volatility. Higher for at-the-money, longer-dated options.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between ITM, ATM, and OTM options?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>In The Money (ITM):</strong> Has intrinsic value. Call: stock price > strike. Put: stock price < strike. More expensive but higher probability of profit.</p><p><strong>At The Money (ATM):</strong> Strike price equals (or is nearest to) the current stock price. Highest time value. Delta ~0.50.</p><p><strong>Out of The Money (OTM):</strong> No intrinsic value. Call: stock price < strike. Put: stock price > strike. Cheaper but lower probability of profit.</p><div class='tip'>Beginners often buy deep OTM options because they're cheap. But they're cheap for a reason - low probability of profit. Start with ATM or slightly ITM options for higher win rates.</div>" },
      { q: "What is a covered call strategy?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>covered call</strong> involves owning 100 shares of stock AND selling a call option against them. It generates income from the premium collected.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Own 100 shares of AAPL at $180. Sell a $190 call expiring in 30 days for $3.00 ($300 premium). If AAPL stays below $190, you keep the premium. If AAPL rises above $190, your shares are called away at $190 + you keep the premium.</p><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Income generation, reduces cost basis, works in flat/slightly bullish markets.</p><p><strong>Cons:</strong> Caps your upside potential, doesn't protect against large downside moves.</p><p>This is one of the most popular options strategies and a great starting point for options sellers. If you're trading covered calls on a regular schedule, automating your entries via TradingView alerts and a platform like PickMyTrade can ensure consistent execution.</p>" },
      { q: "What is an Iron Condor?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>An <strong>Iron Condor</strong> sells an out-of-the-money call spread AND an out-of-the-money put spread simultaneously, profiting when price stays within a range.</p><p><strong>Construction:</strong></p><ul><li>Sell OTM put (lower strike)</li><li>Buy further OTM put (even lower strike - protection)</li><li>Sell OTM call (higher strike)</li><li>Buy further OTM call (even higher strike - protection)</li></ul><p><strong>Max profit:</strong> Net credit received (when price stays between short strikes).</p><p><strong>Max loss:</strong> Width of the wider spread minus credit received.</p><p>Best in: High IV environments (expensive premiums to sell), range-bound markets, stable conditions. Typically target 30-45 DTE and manage at 50% of max profit.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a vertical spread (bull call / bear put)?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>vertical spread</strong> involves buying and selling options of the same type (calls or puts) with the same expiration but different strikes.</p><p><strong>Bull Call Spread:</strong> Buy a call + Sell a higher-strike call. Bullish, limited risk, limited reward. Cheaper than buying a naked call.</p><p><strong>Bear Put Spread:</strong> Buy a put + Sell a lower-strike put. Bearish, limited risk, limited reward. Cheaper than buying a naked put.</p><p><strong>Why use spreads?</strong> They reduce cost (premium paid), define your maximum risk, and reduce the impact of time decay and IV crush compared to single options.</p>" },
      { q: "What is implied volatility crush and how does it affect my options?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>IV Crush</strong> happens when implied volatility drops sharply, typically after an anticipated event (earnings report, FOMC meeting, FDA decision). Even if you get the direction right, your option can lose value because the volatility premium evaporates.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Before earnings, a stock's IV is 80%. You buy a call for $5.00. The stock moves up $2, but IV drops to 40%. Your call might only be worth $3.50 despite the correct direction.</p><p><strong>How to handle it:</strong></p><ul><li>Sell options before events instead of buying (profit from IV crush)</li><li>Use spreads to reduce vega exposure</li><li>If buying, ensure the expected move exceeds the 'expected move' priced into options</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What happens when my option expires?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>At expiration:</p><ul><li><strong>In The Money by $0.01+:</strong> Most brokers auto-exercise. Calls: you buy 100 shares at the strike. Puts: you sell 100 shares at the strike. Make sure you have sufficient margin!</li><li><strong>Out of The Money:</strong> Expires worthless. You lose the premium paid (buyer) or keep the premium (seller)</li><li><strong>At The Money:</strong> Usually expires worthless, but may be exercised by some holders</li></ul><div class='warning'>Auto-exercise can surprise you. If you don't want to be assigned shares, close ITM options before expiration. Many traders set a reminder for expiration week.</div>" },
      { q: "What is a straddle and strangle strategy?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Long Straddle:</strong> Buy a call AND a put at the same strike price (ATM). You profit if price makes a big move in EITHER direction. Cost is high (two premiums), so the move must exceed the total premium paid.</p><p><strong>Long Strangle:</strong> Buy an OTM call AND an OTM put. Cheaper than a straddle but requires an even bigger move to profit. The breakeven points are further apart.</p><p><strong>Best for:</strong> High-volatility events (earnings, FOMC) when you expect a big move but unsure of direction. Careful: IV crush after the event can eat your profits even if the stock moves.</p><p><strong>Short straddle/strangle:</strong> The opposite - sell both sides. Profit from time decay if price stays in a range. High risk if price moves sharply.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the wheel strategy for options?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>wheel strategy</strong> is a systematic approach that combines cash-secured puts and covered calls:</p><ol><li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Sell a cash-secured put on a stock you want to own. Collect premium while waiting</li><li><strong>Step 2:</strong> If assigned (stock drops below strike), you buy shares at a discount (strike price - premium)</li><li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Sell covered calls against your shares. Collect more premium</li><li><strong>Step 4:</strong> If called away (stock rises above call strike), you sell at a profit. Return to Step 1</li></ol><p>The wheel generates income in any market condition and works best on quality stocks with moderate volatility that you'd be happy to own long-term.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a LEAP option?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>LEAPS (Long-Term Equity Anticipation Securities)</strong> are options with expiration dates more than 1 year out. They allow you to control 100 shares for a fraction of the cost.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Instead of buying 100 shares of AAPL at $200 ($20,000), buy a LEAPS call with a $180 strike expiring in 18 months for about $35 ($3,500). You control the same 100 shares for 82.5% less capital.</p><p><strong>Advantages:</strong> Lower capital requirement, defined risk, leverage. <strong>Disadvantages:</strong> Time decay (though slow for LEAPS), premium cost, no dividends.</p><p>LEAPS are popular as stock replacement strategies and as a way to make leveraged bets on long-term trends.</p>" },
      { q: "What is options assignment risk?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Assignment</strong> occurs when an option seller is obligated to fulfill the contract. If you sold a call and the buyer exercises, you must deliver 100 shares at the strike price.</p><p><strong>When assignment happens:</strong></p><ul><li>American-style options (most stock options) can be exercised at any time</li><li>Assignment risk increases when options are deep ITM or near expiration</li><li>Early assignment is more likely when a dividend is approaching (for calls)</li></ul><p><strong>Managing assignment risk:</strong> Close positions before expiration if you don't want assignment. Watch for ex-dividend dates if you're short calls. Keep sufficient margin for potential share delivery.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 19. FUTURES TRADING ───
  {
    category: "Futures Trading",
    icon: "&#128640;",
    color: "rgba(99,179,237,0.12)",
    description: "Contracts, margin, and futures-specific concepts",
    questions: [
      { q: "What are futures contracts and how do they work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>futures contract</strong> is a standardized agreement to buy or sell a specific asset at a predetermined price on a future date. Unlike stocks, futures have expiration dates and use margin (leverage).</p><p><strong>Key features:</strong></p><ul><li>Standardized contract size (e.g., ES = $50 x S&P 500 index)</li><li>Set expiration dates (quarterly for indices: March, June, September, December)</li><li>Daily settlement (profits/losses credited/debited each day)</li><li>No PDT rule (unlike stocks)</li><li>Tax advantages (60/40 long-term/short-term capital gains in the US)</li></ul><p>Futures are traded on exchanges like CME, CBOT, NYMEX for everything from stock indices to oil, gold, corn, and currencies.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between E-mini and Micro E-mini futures?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>They're the same index, just different contract sizes:</p><p><strong>E-mini S&P 500 (ES):</strong></p><ul><li>$50 per point, $12.50 per tick</li><li>~$12,000-15,000 initial margin</li><li>Full-size contract for active traders</li></ul><p><strong>Micro E-mini S&P 500 (MES):</strong></p><ul><li>$5 per point, $1.25 per tick (1/10th of ES)</li><li>~$1,200-1,500 initial margin</li><li>Perfect for beginners and small accounts</li></ul><p>Micros are identical to their E-mini counterparts in every way except size. Start with micros to learn the mechanics before scaling up to E-minis. Both E-mini and Micro contracts can be automated using TradingView + PickMyTrade on platforms like Tradovate and Rithmic.</p>" },
      { q: "What are the most popular futures contracts to trade?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Stock Index Futures:</strong></p><ul><li>ES/MES (S&P 500) - Most liquid futures contract in the world</li><li>NQ/MNQ (Nasdaq-100) - Tech-heavy, more volatile than ES</li><li>YM/MYM (Dow Jones) - Least volatile major index</li><li>RTY/M2K (Russell 2000) - Small-cap, highest volatility</li></ul><p><strong>Commodity Futures:</strong></p><ul><li>CL/MCL (Crude Oil) - Very popular, volatile</li><li>GC/MGC (Gold) - Safe haven, macro trends</li><li>NG (Natural Gas) - Extremely volatile, weather-driven</li></ul><p><strong>Currency/Rate Futures:</strong></p><ul><li>6E (EUR/USD), 6J (JPY), ZB (30-Year Treasury)</li></ul><p>For beginners: start with MES or MNQ. High liquidity, small size, and very well-documented. All of these can be automated from TradingView using PickMyTrade's webhook integration.</p>" },
      { q: "What is contango and backwardation in futures?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Contango:</strong> When futures prices are higher than spot (current) price. The futures curve slopes upward. Normal condition - reflects storage costs, interest rates, and convenience yield.</p><p><strong>Backwardation:</strong> When futures prices are lower than spot price. The curve slopes downward. Can indicate supply shortages or immediate demand pressure.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong></p><ul><li>ETFs that hold futures (like USO for oil, VXX for VIX) lose value in contango because they must 'roll' from cheaper expiring contracts to more expensive new ones</li><li>Backwardation benefits long futures holders via positive roll yield</li><li>Monitoring the term structure can signal market stress or complacency</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the 60/40 tax rule for futures?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Under <strong>IRS Section 1256</strong>, futures profits receive preferential tax treatment: <strong>60% of gains are taxed as long-term capital gains</strong> and <strong>40% as short-term</strong>, regardless of how long you held the position.</p><p>For someone in the 35% tax bracket:</p><ul><li>Stock day trading: 35% flat rate (all short-term)</li><li>Futures day trading: ~23% blended rate (60% at 15% + 40% at 35%)</li></ul><p>This can save thousands in taxes annually. Futures also benefit from mark-to-market accounting - no need to track individual trades for tax purposes. Your broker provides a single Form 1099.</p><div class='tip'>This is one of the biggest advantages of futures over stocks for active traders. Consult a tax professional for your specific situation.</div>" },
      { q: "How does rollover work in futures trading?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Futures contracts expire, so traders must 'roll' to the next contract month to maintain their position. For quarterly index futures (ES, NQ), this happens four times per year.</p><p><strong>Roll process:</strong></p><ul><li>Close your position in the expiring month</li><li>Open the same position in the next month</li><li>Most volume shifts to the new month 7-10 days before expiration</li><li>CME's 'roll dates' are the second Thursday before quarterly expiration</li></ul><p><strong>Important:</strong> The new contract month typically trades at a slightly different price. This is normal and reflects the cost of carry. Most charting platforms offer 'continuous contracts' that adjust for these gaps.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the minimum capital needed to start futures trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The absolute minimum depends on the contract:</p><ul><li><strong>Micro E-mini (MES, MNQ):</strong> Technically ~$1,200-1,500 initial margin. But realistically need $2,500-5,000 to have adequate cushion</li><li><strong>E-mini (ES, NQ):</strong> ~$12,000-15,000 margin. Realistically need $25,000-50,000</li></ul><p><strong>Practical recommendation:</strong> Start with at least $5,000 trading Micro contracts. This gives you room for drawdowns and proper position sizing (not using more than 50% of available margin).</p><div class='warning'>Don't confuse margin requirement with recommended account size. Trading with minimum margin and no buffer is the fastest path to a margin call.</div>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 20. NEWS & ECONOMIC TRADING ───
  {
    category: "News & Economic Trading",
    icon: "&#128240;",
    color: "rgba(104,211,145,0.12)",
    description: "How news, data, and events move markets",
    questions: [
      { q: "How does news affect the stock market?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>News creates rapid repricing of assets as traders incorporate new information. The impact depends on whether the news was <strong>expected or unexpected</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Expected news (already priced in):</strong> 'Buy the rumor, sell the news' - markets often reverse on expected events</li><li><strong>Unexpected news:</strong> Causes the biggest moves. Surprise rate cuts, geopolitical events, unexpected earnings</li></ul><p><strong>High-impact news categories:</strong></p><ul><li>Federal Reserve decisions and speeches</li><li>Jobs reports (NFP - first Friday of each month)</li><li>Inflation data (CPI, PPI)</li><li>GDP reports</li><li>Earnings reports (individual stocks)</li><li>Geopolitical events (wars, trade policies, elections)</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the FOMC and why does it matter for traders?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>Federal Open Market Committee (FOMC)</strong> is the branch of the Federal Reserve that sets US monetary policy, primarily the federal funds rate (interest rates). They meet ~8 times per year.</p><p><strong>Why it moves markets:</strong> Interest rates affect everything - stock valuations, bond prices, currency strength, mortgage rates, corporate borrowing costs. A rate hike makes borrowing expensive (bearish for stocks); a rate cut makes borrowing cheap (bullish).</p><p><strong>Trading FOMC days:</strong></p><ul><li>Decision at 2:00 PM ET, press conference at 2:30 PM ET</li><li>Volatility spikes enormously in the minutes around the announcement</li><li>Many traders avoid holding positions through FOMC. Others trade the post-announcement volatility</li><li>The wording of the statement matters as much as the rate decision itself</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is 'buy the rumor, sell the news' and how do I use it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>This phenomenon occurs when the market prices in an expected event beforehand. When the news actually hits, the buying pressure exhausts and the price reverses.</p><p><strong>Examples:</strong></p><ul><li>A stock rallies 20% into earnings on high expectations, then drops 5% despite beating estimates</li><li>Markets rally in anticipation of a Fed rate cut, then sell off when the cut actually happens</li><li>Bitcoin pumps before a halving event, then corrects after it occurs</li></ul><p><strong>How to use it:</strong> If an asset has already rallied significantly into a known catalyst, be cautious buying more. Consider taking partial profits before the event. The biggest moves often happen <em>before</em> the event, not after.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I trade around earnings reports?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Before earnings:</strong> IV rises as traders buy options for protection/speculation. Stocks often 'walk up' or 'drift down' into earnings based on expectation.</p><p><strong>During earnings:</strong> Massive gap up or down at next open. Options IV crushes immediately.</p><p><strong>Strategies:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pre-earnings:</strong> If bullish, buy stock/calls before. Use defined-risk if playing options (spreads)</li><li><strong>Straddle/Strangle:</strong> Buy both call and put if you expect a big move either way (expensive due to high IV)</li><li><strong>Post-earnings play:</strong> Wait for the gap, then trade the follow-through or fade</li><li><strong>Iron Condor:</strong> Sell if you think the move will be smaller than expected (benefit from IV crush)</li></ul><div class='warning'>The options market prices in the expected move. If market expects a $5 move, the stock must move MORE than $5 for option buyers to profit. Check the 'expected move' before trading.</div>" },
      { q: "What are the most important economic indicators for traders?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Tier 1 (Highest Impact):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Non-Farm Payrolls (NFP):</strong> Monthly jobs data. First Friday. Moves all markets</li><li><strong>Consumer Price Index (CPI):</strong> Monthly inflation data. Directly impacts Fed policy expectations</li><li><strong>FOMC Rate Decision:</strong> Interest rate changes and forward guidance</li><li><strong>GDP:</strong> Quarterly economic growth. Confirms expansion/contraction</li></ul><p><strong>Tier 2 (High Impact):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>PPI (Producer Prices):</strong> Leading indicator for CPI</li><li><strong>Retail Sales:</strong> Consumer spending strength</li><li><strong>ISM Manufacturing/Services:</strong> Business activity health</li><li><strong>Initial Jobless Claims:</strong> Weekly employment pulse</li></ul><p>Use an economic calendar (Forex Factory, Investing.com) to track upcoming releases.</p>" },
      { q: "What is pre-market and after-hours trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Pre-market:</strong> 4:00 AM - 9:30 AM ET. Volume is low, spreads are wide, but this is when stocks react to overnight news, earnings reports, and global market moves.</p><p><strong>After-hours:</strong> 4:00 PM - 8:00 PM ET. Companies often report earnings after the close, causing big moves.</p><p><strong>Key differences from regular hours:</strong></p><ul><li>Lower liquidity = wider spreads and more slippage</li><li>Only limit orders (no market orders at most brokers)</li><li>Prices can be misleading - a big after-hours move may not hold at open</li><li>Not all brokers offer extended hours trading</li></ul><div class='tip'>Use pre-market to observe and plan, not necessarily to trade. Many experienced traders watch pre-market price action but wait for the regular session open to execute.</div>" },
      { q: "What is a gap in stock prices and how do I trade gaps?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>gap</strong> occurs when a stock opens at a significantly different price from its previous close, creating a visible gap on the chart.</p><p><strong>Types of gaps:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Common Gap:</strong> Small, often fills quickly. Trade the fill</li><li><strong>Breakaway Gap:</strong> Opens above/below a consolidation range. Starts a new trend. Don't fight it</li><li><strong>Runaway (Continuation) Gap:</strong> Occurs mid-trend. Confirms strong momentum</li><li><strong>Exhaustion Gap:</strong> Occurs near end of trend on high volume. Often fills = potential reversal</li></ul><p><strong>Gap and Go Strategy:</strong> When a stock gaps up strongly with high volume and relative volume, ride the momentum in the first 30-60 minutes. Set stop below the opening 5-minute low.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 21. FOREX TRADING ───
  {
    category: "Forex Trading",
    icon: "&#128178;",
    color: "rgba(252,129,129,0.12)",
    description: "Currency pairs, pips, and forex-specific concepts",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is forex trading and how does it work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Forex (Foreign Exchange)</strong> is the global marketplace for trading currencies. It's the largest financial market, with over $7.5 trillion traded daily.</p><p>You trade <strong>currency pairs</strong>: when you buy EUR/USD, you're buying Euros and selling US Dollars simultaneously. The first currency (EUR) is the 'base,' the second (USD) is the 'quote.'</p><p><strong>Advantages:</strong> 24/5 trading, high liquidity, low fees (no commissions at most brokers), ability to go long or short easily, no PDT rule.</p><p><strong>Major pairs:</strong> EUR/USD, GBP/USD, USD/JPY, USD/CHF (all involve USD). These have the tightest spreads and most liquidity.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a pip and how much is it worth?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>pip (Percentage in Point)</strong> is the smallest standard unit of price movement:</p><ul><li><strong>Most pairs:</strong> 1 pip = 0.0001 (fourth decimal place). EUR/USD moves from 1.0850 to 1.0851 = 1 pip</li><li><strong>JPY pairs:</strong> 1 pip = 0.01 (second decimal place). USD/JPY moves from 150.00 to 150.01 = 1 pip</li></ul><p><strong>Pip value by lot size:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Standard lot (100,000 units):</strong> ~$10 per pip</li><li><strong>Mini lot (10,000 units):</strong> ~$1 per pip</li><li><strong>Micro lot (1,000 units):</strong> ~$0.10 per pip</li></ul><p>Many brokers also show 'pipettes' - a fifth decimal place (0.00001), which is 1/10th of a pip.</p>" },
      { q: "What is leverage in forex and why is it dangerous?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Forex brokers offer high leverage - up to <strong>50:1 in the US</strong> and up to <strong>500:1</strong> internationally. This means $1,000 can control $50,000+ in currency.</p><p><strong>The danger:</strong> With 50:1 leverage, a 2% move against you wipes out your entire deposit. Currency pairs routinely move 1-2% in a day, sometimes much more.</p><p><strong>Safe leverage use:</strong></p><ul><li>Just because you CAN use 50:1 doesn't mean you SHOULD</li><li>Professional forex traders rarely exceed 5-10:1 effective leverage</li><li>Calculate effective leverage: (Position Size) / (Account Equity)</li><li>Use micro lots when learning to keep effective leverage low</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What are the best times to trade forex?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Forex is open 24/5, but not all hours are equal. The market has four major sessions:</p><ul><li><strong>Sydney:</strong> 5 PM - 2 AM ET (lowest volume)</li><li><strong>Tokyo:</strong> 7 PM - 4 AM ET (JPY pairs most active)</li><li><strong>London:</strong> 3 AM - 12 PM ET (highest volume, EUR/GBP pairs)</li><li><strong>New York:</strong> 8 AM - 5 PM ET (USD pairs most active)</li></ul><p><strong>Best overlap:</strong> London-New York (8 AM - 12 PM ET) has the highest volume, tightest spreads, and biggest moves. Over 50% of all forex transactions occur during this window.</p><p>Avoid trading Sunday evening (low liquidity, wide spreads) and major holiday periods.</p>" },
      { q: "What moves currency pairs the most?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Key drivers of currency movements:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Interest rate differentials:</strong> Higher rates attract foreign capital, strengthening the currency. This is the #1 long-term driver</li><li><strong>Central bank policy:</strong> Hawkish (rate hiking) = currency strength. Dovish (rate cutting) = weakness</li><li><strong>Economic data:</strong> GDP, employment, inflation data relative to expectations</li><li><strong>Geopolitical events:</strong> Wars, trade disputes, elections</li><li><strong>Risk sentiment:</strong> In 'risk-off,' traders flee to safe havens (USD, JPY, CHF). In 'risk-on,' they move to higher-yielding currencies (AUD, NZD)</li><li><strong>Trade balances:</strong> Countries with trade surpluses see currency appreciation over time</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is a swap rate (rollover) in forex?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>swap rate</strong> (or rollover) is the interest you earn or pay for holding a forex position overnight. It's based on the interest rate differential between the two currencies in the pair.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> If you're long AUD/JPY (buying AUD at 4.35% rate, selling JPY at 0.1% rate), you earn a positive swap because AUD has the higher rate. If you're short AUD/JPY, you pay the swap.</p><p>Swaps are applied daily at 5 PM ET (New York close). On Wednesdays, a triple swap is applied (to account for the weekend). <strong>Swap-free accounts</strong> (Islamic accounts) don't charge/pay swaps for religious compliance.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a currency correlation and how do I use it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Currency correlation</strong> measures how two pairs move relative to each other (-1 to +1 scale).</p><p><strong>Common correlations:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>EUR/USD and GBP/USD:</strong> Strongly positive (~0.80). They tend to move together</li><li><strong>EUR/USD and USD/CHF:</strong> Strongly negative (~-0.90). They move opposite</li><li><strong>AUD/USD and NZD/USD:</strong> Strongly positive (~0.85). Both are commodity currencies</li></ul><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> If you're long EUR/USD AND long GBP/USD, you're essentially doubling your USD short exposure. You think you have two independent trades, but you really have one concentrated bet against the dollar. Check correlations before opening multiple forex positions.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 22. CRYPTOCURRENCY ───
  {
    category: "Cryptocurrency Trading",
    icon: "&#128176;",
    color: "rgba(246,173,85,0.12)",
    description: "Bitcoin, altcoins, DeFi, and crypto-specific risks",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is cryptocurrency and how does it differ from traditional assets?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Cryptocurrency</strong> is a digital asset secured by blockchain technology - a decentralized, public ledger that records all transactions.</p><p><strong>Key differences from traditional assets:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>24/7 trading:</strong> Crypto markets never close (including weekends and holidays)</li><li><strong>Decentralized:</strong> No central authority or government control</li><li><strong>High volatility:</strong> Bitcoin can move 5-10% in a day; altcoins can move 20-50%</li><li><strong>Custody:</strong> You can hold your own assets (self-custody) without a broker</li><li><strong>Regulation:</strong> Still evolving. Tax treatment varies by country</li><li><strong>Liquidity:</strong> Top coins (BTC, ETH) are very liquid. Small altcoins can be extremely illiquid</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between Bitcoin and altcoins?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Bitcoin (BTC):</strong> The first and largest cryptocurrency by market cap. Primarily used as a 'digital gold' store of value. Has the strongest network security and longest track record.</p><p><strong>Altcoins:</strong> Every other cryptocurrency besides Bitcoin. Major categories:</p><ul><li><strong>Smart Contract Platforms:</strong> Ethereum (ETH), Solana (SOL), Avalanche (AVAX)</li><li><strong>DeFi Tokens:</strong> Aave, Uniswap, Maker - power decentralized finance apps</li><li><strong>Stablecoins:</strong> USDT, USDC - pegged to $1, used for trading and parking funds</li><li><strong>Meme Coins:</strong> DOGE, SHIB - speculative, community-driven</li></ul><p>In crypto, BTC often leads the market. When BTC rises, altcoins typically rise more (higher beta). When BTC drops, altcoins drop more.</p>" },
      { q: "What is DeFi and how does it work?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>DeFi (Decentralized Finance)</strong> replaces traditional financial intermediaries (banks, brokers) with smart contracts on blockchains, primarily Ethereum.</p><p><strong>Key DeFi activities:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Lending/Borrowing:</strong> Deposit crypto to earn interest or borrow against your holdings (Aave, Compound)</li><li><strong>DEX Trading:</strong> Swap tokens without a central exchange (Uniswap, SushiSwap)</li><li><strong>Yield Farming:</strong> Provide liquidity to protocols in exchange for token rewards</li><li><strong>Staking:</strong> Lock tokens to help secure a proof-of-stake network and earn rewards</li></ul><div class='warning'>DeFi carries unique risks: smart contract bugs, impermanent loss, rug pulls, regulatory uncertainty. Never invest more than you can afford to lose, and always research protocols before depositing funds.</div>" },
      { q: "What is staking vs. yield farming?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Staking:</strong> Lock your tokens to support a proof-of-stake blockchain's security. Earn predictable rewards (typically 3-12% APY). Lower risk - your tokens stay in a well-known protocol.</p><p><strong>Yield Farming:</strong> Provide liquidity to DeFi protocols (lending platforms, DEXes) for higher returns. More complex, higher risk, and requires active management. Returns can range from 10% to 1000%+ APY, but high yields often come with high risks including impermanent loss and smart contract risks.</p><p><strong>Key risk:</strong> Impermanent loss occurs when the price ratio of your pooled tokens changes, potentially leaving you with less value than simply holding.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a crypto halving and why does it affect prices?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>halving</strong> is an event where the reward for mining new blocks is cut in half, reducing the rate at which new coins are created. Bitcoin halvings occur approximately every 4 years.</p><p><strong>Bitcoin halving history:</strong></p><ul><li>2012: Reward cut from 50 to 25 BTC. Price went from ~$12 to ~$1,100 in the following year</li><li>2016: Reward cut from 25 to 12.5 BTC. Price went from ~$650 to ~$19,000</li><li>2020: Reward cut from 12.5 to 6.25 BTC. Price went from ~$8,700 to ~$69,000</li><li>2024: Reward cut from 6.25 to 3.125 BTC</li></ul><p>Halvings reduce supply issuance while demand can remain constant or increase, creating upward price pressure. However, each successive halving has a diminishing impact as the supply reduction becomes proportionally smaller.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a crypto wallet and what's the difference between hot and cold wallets?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>crypto wallet</strong> stores your private keys (not the crypto itself - that lives on the blockchain). Your keys prove ownership and allow you to send transactions.</p><ul><li><strong>Hot wallet (software):</strong> Connected to the internet. Convenient for trading. Examples: MetaMask, Trust Wallet, exchange wallets (Coinbase, Kraken). Higher risk of hacking</li><li><strong>Cold wallet (hardware):</strong> Offline storage device. Most secure for long-term holding. Examples: Ledger, Trezor. Protects against online attacks</li><li><strong>Paper wallet:</strong> Private key printed/written on paper. Immune to hacking but can be lost or damaged</li></ul><p><strong>Best practice:</strong> Keep trading funds in a hot wallet or exchange, but move long-term holdings to a cold wallet. 'Not your keys, not your crypto.'</p>" },
      { q: "What is a rug pull and how do I avoid crypto scams?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>rug pull</strong> is when crypto developers abandon a project and run away with investor funds. It's one of the most common crypto scams.</p><p><strong>Red flags:</strong></p><ul><li>Anonymous team with no verifiable track record</li><li>Unrealistic promises (1000% APY, guaranteed returns)</li><li>Liquidity locked for very short periods or not at all</li><li>No audit of the smart contract</li><li>Aggressive marketing with no working product</li><li>Concentrated token ownership (few wallets holding most supply)</li></ul><p><strong>Protection:</strong> Research the team, read the smart contract audit, check lock periods, use tools like TokenSniffer or RugDoc to analyze tokens. If it sounds too good to be true, it absolutely is.</p>" },
      { q: "What are NFTs and do they have trading value?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>NFTs (Non-Fungible Tokens)</strong> are unique digital assets on a blockchain representing ownership of items like art, music, collectibles, or virtual real estate.</p><p>Unlike Bitcoin (where every BTC is identical/fungible), each NFT is unique. They're traded on marketplaces like OpenSea, Blur, and Magic Eden.</p><p><strong>As investments:</strong> NFTs are highly speculative. The market saw massive hype in 2021-2022, followed by a severe crash. Most NFTs lose 90%+ of their value. A small number (CryptoPunks, Bored Apes) retain value due to cultural significance and community.</p><div class='warning'>Treat NFTs as highly speculative assets. Never invest more than you can afford to lose completely. The market is illiquid - you may not be able to sell when you want to.</div>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 23. MARKET STRUCTURE & ADVANCED CONCEPTS ───
  {
    category: "Market Structure & Advanced",
    icon: "&#127959;",
    color: "rgba(183,148,244,0.12)",
    description: "Smart money, institutional concepts, and advanced analysis",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is market structure and how do I identify trends?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Market structure</strong> is the pattern of price movement defined by swing highs and swing lows.</p><ul><li><strong>Uptrend:</strong> Series of higher highs (HH) and higher lows (HL)</li><li><strong>Downtrend:</strong> Series of lower highs (LH) and lower lows (LL)</li><li><strong>Sideways/Range:</strong> Equal highs and equal lows</li></ul><p><strong>Trend change signals:</strong></p><ul><li>In an uptrend: when price breaks below the most recent higher low, the structure shifts bearish</li><li>In a downtrend: when price breaks above the most recent lower high, the structure shifts bullish</li></ul><p>Understanding market structure is the foundation of all price action trading. Before using any indicator, learn to read the raw structure of price.</p>" },
      { q: "What are Smart Money Concepts (SMC)?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Smart Money Concepts (SMC)</strong> is a trading framework that focuses on identifying and trading alongside institutional players ('smart money') rather than against them.</p><p><strong>Core concepts:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Order Blocks:</strong> The last opposing candle before a strong move. Marks where institutions placed large orders. Price often returns to these zones</li><li><strong>Fair Value Gaps (FVG):</strong> Three-candle pattern where the middle candle's body doesn't overlap with the first and third candles' wicks. Shows imbalance that price may fill</li><li><strong>Liquidity:</strong> Stop-losses clustered above/below swing points. Institutions target these to fill large orders</li><li><strong>Break of Structure (BOS):</strong> Continuation signal within a trend</li><li><strong>Change of Character (CHoCH):</strong> First sign of trend reversal</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the Wyckoff Method?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>The <strong>Wyckoff Method</strong> is a century-old framework for understanding how institutional players accumulate and distribute positions. It divides market cycles into four phases:</p><ul><li><strong>Accumulation:</strong> Smart money quietly buys from the public after a downtrend. Price moves sideways in a range with specific 'tests' (springs, shakeouts)</li><li><strong>Markup:</strong> The resulting uptrend after accumulation is complete</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Smart money sells to the public after an uptrend. Similar range structure but inverted (upthrusts instead of springs)</li><li><strong>Markdown:</strong> The resulting downtrend after distribution</li></ul><p><strong>Key Wyckoff principle:</strong> Effort vs. Result. High volume without price progress suggests the current move is weakening. This is one of the most reliable signals in all of trading.</p>" },
      { q: "What is Elliott Wave Theory?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Elliott Wave Theory</strong> proposes that markets move in predictable 5-3 wave patterns driven by collective psychology. The pattern consists of 5 impulse waves (with the trend) followed by 3 corrective waves (against the trend).</p><p><strong>Three cardinal rules:</strong></p><ul><li>Wave 2 never retraces more than 100% of Wave 1</li><li>Wave 3 cannot be the shortest impulse wave</li><li>Wave 4 cannot overlap with Wave 1's territory</li></ul><p><strong>Common corrective patterns:</strong> Zigzag (sharp correction), Flat (sideways), Triangle (narrowing range).</p><p>Elliott Wave is subjective and requires significant practice. It works best on higher timeframes (daily, weekly) and is most useful for understanding where you are in a market cycle rather than for pinpoint entries.</p>" },
      { q: "What are order blocks and how do I trade them?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>An <strong>order block</strong> is the last candle moving in the opposite direction before a strong impulsive move. Theory: this is where institutional players placed their orders, creating a supply/demand zone.</p><p><strong>Bullish order block:</strong> The last bearish (red) candle before a strong rally. When price returns to this zone, institutions may add to their positions, creating a bounce.</p><p><strong>Bearish order block:</strong> The last bullish (green) candle before a strong sell-off. Price returning here may face selling pressure.</p><p><strong>Trading approach:</strong></p><ul><li>Identify the order block on a higher timeframe</li><li>Wait for price to return to the order block zone</li><li>Look for confirmation (rejection candle, break of structure on lower timeframe)</li><li>Enter with a stop beyond the order block</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What are fair value gaps (FVG) and why do they fill?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>A <strong>Fair Value Gap (FVG)</strong> or imbalance is a three-candle pattern where the wicks of candles 1 and 3 don't overlap, leaving a 'gap' in traded prices. The middle candle moved so fast that not all price levels were properly traded.</p><p><strong>Why they fill:</strong> Markets seek equilibrium. When price moves too fast in one direction, there are unfilled orders left behind. Price tends to return to these inefficient zones to 'rebalance.'</p><p><strong>Not all FVGs fill:</strong> In strong trends, many FVGs remain unfilled. Use context: FVGs near order blocks or key levels are more likely to be revisited. Higher timeframe FVGs are more significant than lower timeframe ones.</p>" },
      { q: "What is dark pool trading and how does it affect retail traders?", level: "expert", a: "<p><strong>Dark pools</strong> are private exchanges where large institutional orders are matched without being displayed on public order books. They account for 35-45% of total US equity volume.</p><p><strong>Impact on retail traders:</strong></p><ul><li>The Level 2 data you see is incomplete - roughly half the volume is hidden</li><li>Large dark pool prints can signal institutional interest at specific prices</li><li>Dark pool data services (like Quandl, Ortex) show aggregate dark pool activity with a delay</li></ul><p><strong>How to use dark pool data:</strong> Look for unusual dark pool activity (large blocks at specific prices). If a stock has heavy dark pool buying at $50, that level may act as strong support. But dark pool data is delayed, so it's a confirming tool, not a leading signal.</p>" },
      { q: "What is intermarket analysis?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Intermarket analysis</strong> studies how different asset classes influence each other. No market moves in isolation.</p><p><strong>Key relationships:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dollar up = Commodities down:</strong> Most commodities are priced in USD</li><li><strong>Dollar up = Emerging markets down:</strong> Their USD-denominated debt becomes more expensive</li><li><strong>Bonds down (yields up) = Stocks pressured:</strong> Higher rates increase discount rates and borrowing costs</li><li><strong>Gold up = Fear/inflation rising:</strong> Safe haven demand</li><li><strong>Oil up = Inflation expectations rising:</strong> Energy costs feed into all prices</li><li><strong>VIX up = Stocks down:</strong> Almost always inversely correlated</li></ul><p>Understanding these relationships gives you a macro edge. If the dollar is surging, don't go long gold. If bonds are crashing, be cautious with growth stocks.</p>" },
      { q: "What is sector rotation and how do I use it?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Sector rotation</strong> is the practice of shifting capital between stock market sectors based on the economic cycle. Different sectors outperform at different stages:</p><ul><li><strong>Early Recovery:</strong> Technology, Consumer Discretionary, Industrials lead</li><li><strong>Mid Expansion:</strong> Energy, Materials, Industrials perform well</li><li><strong>Late Expansion:</strong> Energy, Staples begin outperforming. Growth slows</li><li><strong>Recession:</strong> Utilities, Healthcare, Consumer Staples (defensive sectors) outperform</li></ul><p><strong>How to track:</strong> Compare sector ETFs (XLK, XLE, XLU, etc.) on a relative strength chart against SPY. Money flows from underperforming to outperforming sectors. Where money is flowing tells you where we are in the cycle.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 24. TRADING TAXES & LEGAL ───
  {
    category: "Taxes & Legal",
    icon: "&#128220;",
    color: "rgba(99,179,237,0.12)",
    description: "Tax implications and legal requirements",
    questions: [
      { q: "How are trading profits taxed in the US?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Trading profits are subject to <strong>capital gains tax</strong>. The rate depends on how long you held the position:</p><ul><li><strong>Short-term capital gains</strong> (held < 1 year): Taxed as ordinary income (10-37% depending on bracket)</li><li><strong>Long-term capital gains</strong> (held > 1 year): Preferential rates of 0%, 15%, or 20%</li><li><strong>Futures (Section 1256):</strong> 60/40 split - 60% long-term, 40% short-term regardless of holding period</li></ul><p>Day traders pay the highest rates since all gains are short-term. This is another reason futures are popular with active traders.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the wash sale rule?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>wash sale rule</strong> prevents you from claiming a tax loss if you buy the same or 'substantially identical' security within 30 days before or after the sale.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> You sell AAPL at a $1,000 loss on March 15. If you buy AAPL again between February 13 - April 14, the loss is disallowed for tax purposes.</p><p><strong>Important details:</strong></p><ul><li>Applies across ALL your accounts (taxable, IRA, spouse's accounts)</li><li>'Substantially identical' includes options on the same stock</li><li>The disallowed loss is added to the cost basis of the new shares (not permanently lost)</li><li>Does NOT apply to futures (Section 1256 contracts)</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is tax-loss harvesting?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Tax-loss harvesting</strong> is selling losing positions to offset capital gains and reduce your tax bill.</p><p><strong>Rules:</strong></p><ul><li>Capital losses first offset capital gains dollar-for-dollar</li><li>Excess losses can offset up to $3,000 of ordinary income per year</li><li>Remaining losses carry forward to future tax years indefinitely</li></ul><p><strong>Strategy:</strong> In December, review your portfolio for unrealized losses. Sell losers to offset winners. Wait 31 days before repurchasing to avoid the wash sale rule. In the meantime, you can buy a similar (but not identical) asset to maintain market exposure.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the Pattern Day Trader (PDT) rule?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>PDT rule</strong> (FINRA Rule 4210) requires a minimum of $25,000 equity in your margin account if you make 4+ day trades within 5 business days (where day trades represent more than 6% of total trades).</p><p><strong>Key points:</strong></p><ul><li>Only applies to margin accounts, NOT cash accounts</li><li>Only applies to stocks and options, NOT futures or forex</li><li>If flagged as PDT without $25,000, your account is restricted for 90 days</li></ul><p><strong>Workarounds:</strong></p><ul><li>Trade futures (no PDT rule, tax advantages)</li><li>Use a cash account (but must wait for settlement T+1)</li><li>Use multiple broker accounts</li><li>Swing trade instead (hold overnight)</li></ul>" },
      { q: "Do I need to report crypto trades on my taxes?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Yes.</strong> The IRS treats cryptocurrency as property. Every sale, trade, or exchange is a taxable event.</p><p><strong>Taxable events:</strong></p><ul><li>Selling crypto for USD</li><li>Trading one crypto for another (e.g., BTC to ETH)</li><li>Using crypto to buy goods/services</li><li>Earning crypto through mining, staking, or airdrops (taxed as income at fair market value)</li></ul><p><strong>NOT taxable:</strong> Buying crypto with USD and holding it, transferring between your own wallets.</p><p>Use crypto tax software (CoinTracker, TaxBit, Koinly) to track your trades. Many exchanges provide tax reports, but they may not capture all your activity across multiple platforms.</p>" },
      { q: "Should I set up an LLC or S-Corp for trading?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>For most retail traders, the answer is <strong>no - not until you're consistently profitable</strong> and trading significant volume.</p><p><strong>When it makes sense:</strong></p><ul><li>You're making $50,000+ in annual trading profits</li><li>You want to deduct trading-related expenses (software, data, education, home office)</li><li>You want to elect Trader Tax Status (TTS) for mark-to-market accounting</li><li>You trade as a full-time business</li></ul><p><strong>Trader Tax Status benefits:</strong> Trade expenses are fully deductible, no wash sale rule with mark-to-market election, can deduct losses against ordinary income without the $3,000 cap.</p><div class='tip'>Consult a tax professional who specializes in trader taxation (e.g., GreenTraderTax). The rules are complex and doing it wrong can trigger IRS scrutiny.</div>" },
      { q: "What trading expenses can I deduct on my taxes?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>If you qualify for <strong>Trader Tax Status (TTS)</strong>, you can deduct:</p><ul><li><strong>Platform/software:</strong> TradingView, NinjaTrader, Bloomberg terminal subscriptions</li><li><strong>Market data feeds:</strong> Real-time quotes, Level 2 data, news services</li><li><strong>Education:</strong> Trading courses, books, seminars (if directly related to your trading business)</li><li><strong>Home office:</strong> Dedicated space used exclusively for trading (pro-rated)</li><li><strong>Computer equipment:</strong> Monitors, computers, internet (business % deductible)</li><li><strong>Commissions and fees:</strong> These reduce your capital gains automatically on Schedule D</li></ul><p>Without TTS, only commissions/fees reduce gains. Other expenses are NOT deductible for hobbyist traders under current tax law.</p>" },
      { q: "How are prop firm payouts taxed?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>This is a <strong>gray area in tax law</strong> that varies by prop firm structure:</p><ul><li><strong>If you're an independent contractor:</strong> Profits are reported on 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC. You pay self-employment tax (15.3%) plus income tax. Can deduct business expenses on Schedule C</li><li><strong>If the firm trades on their capital:</strong> Your share may be treated as fee income or profit sharing</li><li><strong>International prop firms:</strong> May not issue US tax forms. You're still required to report worldwide income</li></ul><p><strong>Important:</strong> Prop firm profits are generally NOT eligible for Section 1256 (60/40) treatment because you don't own the positions. Keep detailed records and consult a tax advisor familiar with prop firm structures.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 25. ALGORITHMIC & AUTOMATED TRADING ───
  {
    category: "Algorithmic & Automated Trading",
    icon: "&#129302;",
    color: "rgba(79,209,197,0.12)",
    description: "Bots, backtesting, and trading automation",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is algorithmic trading?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Algorithmic trading (algo trading)</strong> uses computer programs to execute trades based on predefined rules. The algorithm analyzes market data, identifies opportunities, and places orders automatically - often faster than any human could.</p><p>Algo trading accounts for <strong>over 60% of all US stock trades</strong>. It ranges from simple (buy when RSI < 30) to incredibly complex (machine learning models processing millions of data points).</p><p><strong>Benefits:</strong> Removes emotion, executes at optimal speed, can monitor many markets simultaneously, backtestable.</p><p><strong>Challenges:</strong> Requires programming knowledge, needs robust backtesting to avoid curve-fitting, needs infrastructure and monitoring.</p>" },
      { q: "What is backtesting and why is it essential?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Backtesting</strong> applies your trading strategy to historical data to see how it would have performed. It's the scientific method applied to trading.</p><p><strong>What good backtesting includes:</strong></p><ul><li>Sufficient data (minimum 2-3 years, ideally 5-10 years)</li><li>Multiple market conditions (bull, bear, sideways, volatile, calm)</li><li>Realistic assumptions: commissions, slippage, spread</li><li>Out-of-sample testing (don't optimize on ALL data - hold some back for validation)</li></ul><div class='warning'><strong>Curve fitting trap:</strong> Over-optimizing parameters to fit historical data perfectly creates a strategy that works in the past but fails in live trading. If your backtest looks 'too good to be true,' it probably is. Simpler strategies with fewer parameters tend to be more robust.</div>" },
      { q: "How do I automate my TradingView strategy?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>TradingView doesn't execute trades directly, but you can automate via <strong>webhook alerts</strong>:</p><ol><li>Create your strategy in Pine Script on TradingView</li><li>Set up an alert with the webhook URL of your automation platform</li><li>The platform receives the alert and places the trade on your broker</li></ol><p><strong>Automation platforms:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>PickMyTrade:</strong> Connects TradingView to Tradovate, Rithmic, Interactive Brokers, TradeStation, and more. No coding required</li><li><strong>TradersPost:</strong> TradingView to various brokers</li><li><strong>Custom webhook servers:</strong> Build your own (requires programming)</li></ul><p>You need a TradingView plan that supports webhook alerts (Pro, Pro+, or Premium).</p>" },
      { q: "What programming languages are best for trading bots?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Python</strong> is the most popular choice for retail algo traders. Simple syntax, massive library ecosystem (pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn, backtrader), great for prototyping and backtesting.</p><p><strong>Other options:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pine Script:</strong> TradingView's built-in language. Easy to learn, great for strategy development and alerts</li><li><strong>C++/C#:</strong> Used by HFT firms for speed. Overkill for most retail traders</li><li><strong>JavaScript/Node.js:</strong> Good for building webhook servers and web-based tools</li><li><strong>MQL4/5:</strong> MetaTrader's language for forex bots (Expert Advisors)</li><li><strong>R:</strong> Strong for statistical analysis and research, less common for execution</li></ul><p>For most traders, <strong>Pine Script + a webhook automation platform</strong> is the fastest path to automated trading without deep programming knowledge.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between backtesting and forward testing?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Backtesting:</strong> Testing against historical data. Fast, comprehensive, but subject to look-ahead bias and curve fitting.</p><p><strong>Forward testing (paper trading / incubation):</strong> Running your strategy in real-time on live market data with simulated money. Slower (takes weeks/months) but reveals issues backtesting can't: execution quality, slippage, connectivity, emotional response to drawdowns.</p><p><strong>Proper development flow:</strong></p><ol><li>Backtest across multiple market conditions</li><li>Out-of-sample validation</li><li>Forward test for 2-3 months minimum</li><li>Go live with small position sizes</li><li>Scale up gradually as confidence builds</li></ol>" },
      { q: "What is high-frequency trading (HFT)?", level: "expert", a: "<p><strong>High-Frequency Trading</strong> uses ultra-fast computers to execute thousands of orders per second, holding positions for microseconds to milliseconds. HFT firms invest millions in co-located servers (placed physically next to exchange servers) and custom hardware for nanosecond advantages.</p><p><strong>Common HFT strategies:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Market Making:</strong> Providing liquidity on both sides, profiting from the spread</li><li><strong>Statistical Arbitrage:</strong> Exploiting tiny price discrepancies between correlated instruments</li><li><strong>Latency Arbitrage:</strong> Acting on price differences between exchanges before they equalize</li></ul><p>HFT is <strong>not accessible to retail traders</strong>. The infrastructure costs alone run into millions. Retail traders should focus on strategies that operate on longer timeframes where HFT's speed advantage doesn't apply.</p>" },
      { q: "What metrics should I track for my automated strategy?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Essential performance metrics:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Net P&L:</strong> Total profit after all costs (commissions, slippage, data fees)</li><li><strong>Win Rate:</strong> Percentage of profitable trades</li><li><strong>Profit Factor:</strong> Gross profit / Gross loss. Above 1.5 is good; above 2.0 is excellent</li><li><strong>Sharpe Ratio:</strong> Risk-adjusted return. Above 1.0 is acceptable; above 2.0 is very good</li><li><strong>Maximum Drawdown:</strong> Largest peak-to-trough decline. Keep under 20% for peace of mind</li><li><strong>Expectancy:</strong> (Win% x Avg Win) - (Loss% x Avg Loss). Must be positive for profitability</li><li><strong>Average R-Multiple:</strong> Average profit/loss measured in units of risk</li><li><strong>Recovery Factor:</strong> Net profit / Max drawdown. Shows how quickly the strategy recovers from losses</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is overfitting in trading strategy development?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Overfitting (curve fitting)</strong> occurs when a strategy is optimized so heavily on historical data that it captures noise rather than genuine market patterns. The strategy looks amazing in backtests but fails in live trading.</p><p><strong>Signs of overfitting:</strong></p><ul><li>Strategy has many parameters (>5-7 is a red flag)</li><li>Backtest results are unrealistically good (Sharpe > 3, drawdown < 5%)</li><li>Performance degrades significantly with slight parameter changes</li><li>Works only on one specific time period or market</li></ul><p><strong>Prevention:</strong></p><ul><li>Keep strategies simple (fewer parameters)</li><li>Use out-of-sample testing (train on 70% of data, test on remaining 30%)</li><li>Walk-forward analysis (sliding window optimization)</li><li>Test on multiple instruments and timeframes</li><li>If it looks too good to be true, it is</li></ul>" },
      { q: "How do I handle internet outages or system failures during automated trading?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>System reliability is critical</strong> for automated trading. A disconnection during an open position can lead to uncontrolled losses.</p><p><strong>Safeguards:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Broker-side stops:</strong> Always have stop-loss orders on the broker's server, not just your local software</li><li><strong>VPS (Virtual Private Server):</strong> Run your bot on a cloud server near the exchange. Eliminates home internet dependency</li><li><strong>Mobile alerts:</strong> Set up push notifications for trade executions and system errors</li><li><strong>Maximum daily loss:</strong> Configure a 'kill switch' that flattens all positions if daily loss exceeds a threshold</li><li><strong>Redundancy:</strong> Backup internet connection, UPS for power outages, ability to manage positions from a phone</li></ul>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 26. TRADING BOT DEVELOPMENT ───
  {
    category: "Trading Bot Development",
    icon: "&#128187;",
    color: "rgba(246,173,85,0.12)",
    description: "Common issues from GitHub repos (Freqtrade, CCXT, Backtrader)",
    questions: [
      { q: "Why does my trading bot perform differently in backtesting vs. live trading?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>This is the <strong>#1 complaint on GitHub trading repos</strong> (Freqtrade #10638, Backtrader forums). Common causes:</p><ul><li><strong>Look-ahead bias:</strong> Your backtest uses data that wasn't available at trade time (e.g., using the close price to make a decision that should use the open)</li><li><strong>Slippage modeling:</strong> Backtests often assume perfect fills. Live markets have slippage, especially in fast-moving or illiquid markets</li><li><strong>Indicator repainting:</strong> Some indicators change their historical values as new data arrives (common in Pine Script)</li><li><strong>Timing differences:</strong> Backtests process candles sequentially; live systems process them in real-time with potential delays</li><li><strong>Survivorship bias:</strong> Historical data may not include delisted stocks or failed tokens</li></ul><div class='tip'>Always forward-test (paper trade) for 2-3 months before going live. If backtest shows 80% win rate and live shows 50%, the strategy is likely overfit.</div>" },
      { q: "How do I prevent API rate limiting when building a trading bot?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Exchanges and data providers limit API calls to prevent abuse. Exceeding limits causes errors and missed trades. Common from CCXT GitHub issues and yfinance #2422.</p><p><strong>Solutions:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Respect rate limits:</strong> Check your exchange's documentation — most cap requests per minute, per endpoint</li><li><strong>Batch requests:</strong> Fetch multiple symbols in one call instead of one per symbol</li><li><strong>Use WebSockets:</strong> For real-time data, WebSocket connections push data to you instead of you polling</li><li><strong>Cache data:</strong> Store fetched data locally and refresh at intervals, not on every tick</li><li><strong>Implement exponential backoff:</strong> If rate-limited, wait 1s, then 2s, then 4s before retrying</li><li><strong>Reduce frequency:</strong> If trading on 1h candles, you don't need to check every second</li></ul>" },
      { q: "How do I secure my trading bot API keys?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>API key security is critical - <strong>leaked keys have caused massive losses</strong>. GitHub warns that any exposed keys should be treated as compromised immediately.</p><p><strong>Best practices:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Never commit keys to Git:</strong> Use .env files and add .env to .gitignore</li><li><strong>Use environment variables:</strong> Load keys from OS environment, not hardcoded strings</li><li><strong>IP whitelist:</strong> Most exchanges let you restrict API key usage to specific IP addresses</li><li><strong>Minimize permissions:</strong> If your bot only trades, don't enable withdrawal permissions</li><li><strong>Use subaccounts:</strong> Many exchanges support separate API keys for subaccounts with limited funds</li><li><strong>Rotate keys regularly:</strong> Change API keys every few months as a precaution</li></ul><div class='warning'>If you accidentally push API keys to a public GitHub repo, revoke them immediately. Bots scan GitHub in real-time for leaked crypto exchange keys.</div>" },
      { q: "What is slippage in automated trading and how do I model it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Slippage</strong> is the difference between the price your bot expects and the price it actually gets. From Freqtrade issue #3524, implementing slippage filters is crucial for realistic backtesting.</p><p><strong>Modeling slippage in backtests:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Fixed slippage:</strong> Add 1-2 ticks to each fill (simple but crude)</li><li><strong>Percentage-based:</strong> Add 0.05-0.1% to each trade cost</li><li><strong>Volume-based:</strong> Calculate slippage from historical orderbook data and position size</li><li><strong>Orderbook simulation:</strong> Use Level 2 data to estimate actual fill prices based on available liquidity</li></ul><p>A strategy that's barely profitable in backtest will likely be unprofitable live after accounting for real slippage. Add realistic slippage to your backtests from day one.</p>" },
      { q: "What are common webhook issues when automating TradingView?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>From GitHub issues and TradingView community forums, the most common webhook problems:</p><ul><li><strong>Missed webhooks:</strong> TradingView doesn't guarantee delivery. Implement retry logic on your server</li><li><strong>Duplicate alerts:</strong> The same alert can fire multiple times. Use unique IDs and idempotent order handling</li><li><strong>JSON formatting errors:</strong> Malformed alert messages cause silent failures. Test your payload format thoroughly</li><li><strong>SSL/HTTPS requirement:</strong> TradingView requires HTTPS endpoints. Use services like ngrok for local testing or deploy on a cloud server</li><li><strong>Alert message size:</strong> TradingView has a character limit. Keep payloads concise</li><li><strong>Timing:</strong> Webhook delivery can be delayed 1-5 seconds. Plan your strategy accordingly</li></ul><p><strong>Recommended approach:</strong> Use an established platform like PickMyTrade that handles webhook reliability, duplicate detection, and broker connectivity out of the box.</p>" },
      { q: "What open-source trading frameworks are available on GitHub?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Popular frameworks from the <strong>awesome-quant</strong> repository and GitHub Topics:</p><p><strong>Backtesting:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Backtrader (Python):</strong> Feature-rich, event-driven. Great documentation. 11k+ stars</li><li><strong>Backtesting.py:</strong> Lightweight, fast. Good for quick prototyping</li><li><strong>Zipline (Python):</strong> Originally by Quantopian. Production-grade but less actively maintained</li></ul><p><strong>Live Trading:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Freqtrade:</strong> Crypto trading bot with backtesting, hyperopt, and Telegram integration. 30k+ stars</li><li><strong>CCXT:</strong> Unified API for 100+ crypto exchanges. Essential for multi-exchange bots</li><li><strong>QuantConnect/Lean:</strong> Professional-grade algo trading engine. Python & C#</li></ul><p><strong>Data & Analysis:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>yfinance:</strong> Free stock data from Yahoo Finance</li><li><strong>TA-Lib:</strong> Technical analysis library with 200+ indicators</li><li><strong>PyPortfolioOpt:</strong> Portfolio optimization (mean-variance, Black-Litterman)</li></ul>" },
      { q: "Why does my Pine Script indicator repaint?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Repainting</strong> occurs when an indicator changes its past values as new data arrives. This makes backtests look unrealistically profitable because the indicator 'knew' the future.</p><p><strong>Common causes:</strong></p><ul><li>Using <code>security()</code> function on a lower timeframe without <code>lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off</code></li><li>Using real-time bar data (the current, unclosed bar's high/low/close changes with each tick)</li><li>Certain built-in functions like <code>pivothigh()</code> and <code>pivotlow()</code> look ahead by default</li></ul><p><strong>How to detect:</strong> Add your indicator to a chart and watch if historical signals change when you refresh. Or compare 'strategy' backtest results with real-time alert behavior.</p><p><strong>How to fix:</strong> Use <code>barstate.isconfirmed</code> to only signal on closed bars. Avoid real-time bar data for decision-making. Always test with <code>calc_on_every_tick=false</code>.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a VPS and why do automated traders need one?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>VPS (Virtual Private Server)</strong> is a remote server running 24/7 in a data center. For automated trading, it ensures your bot runs continuously without depending on your home computer or internet.</p><p><strong>Why you need one:</strong></p><ul><li>Your home PC might crash, lose power, or lose internet during a trade</li><li>VPS uptime is typically 99.9%+ with redundant power and connectivity</li><li>Lower latency if you choose a VPS near your broker's servers</li><li>Run your bot while your PC is off</li></ul><p><strong>Popular choices:</strong> AWS, DigitalOcean, Vultr for general use. ForexVPS and BeeksFX for trading-optimized servers. Costs range from $5-50/month depending on specs.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I test a webhook integration without real money?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Always test webhooks in a risk-free environment first:</p><ol><li><strong>Use webhook testing tools:</strong> Sites like webhook.site or RequestBin let you see if TradingView is sending alerts correctly</li><li><strong>Paper trading mode:</strong> Most automation platforms (including PickMyTrade) support paper trading / simulation mode</li><li><strong>Broker simulation accounts:</strong> Tradovate offers sim accounts, NinjaTrader has sim mode, most brokers have paper trading</li><li><strong>Log everything:</strong> Record every webhook received, every order placed, every fill. Compare with expected behavior</li><li><strong>Test edge cases:</strong> What happens if the webhook fires twice? What if the market is closed? What if the price has moved significantly?</li></ol><div class='tip'>Never skip this step. A bug in your webhook integration can place unintended trades with real money.</div>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 27. SHORT SELLING & ADVANCED STRATEGIES ───
  {
    category: "Short Selling & Advanced Strategies",
    icon: "&#128201;",
    color: "rgba(252,129,129,0.12)",
    description: "Shorting, pair trading, and sophisticated techniques",
    questions: [
      { q: "How does short selling work?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Short selling</strong> is selling shares you don't own (borrowed from your broker) with the plan to buy them back later at a lower price.</p><p><strong>Process:</strong></p><ol><li>Borrow shares from your broker's inventory</li><li>Sell them at the current market price</li><li>Wait for the price to drop</li><li>Buy back ('cover') at a lower price</li><li>Return shares to the lender, pocket the difference</li></ol><p><strong>Example:</strong> Short 100 shares at $50. Price drops to $40. Buy back at $40. Profit = ($50 - $40) x 100 = $1,000, minus borrowing costs.</p><div class='warning'>Unlike going long (max loss = 100% if stock goes to $0), short selling has <strong>theoretically unlimited loss potential</strong>. If a $50 stock rises to $500, you lose $450/share. Always use stops when shorting.</div>" },
      { q: "What is a short squeeze and how can I identify one?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>short squeeze</strong> occurs when a heavily shorted stock rises, forcing short sellers to buy back shares (cover), which pushes the price even higher, triggering more covering in a cascading effect.</p><p><strong>Conditions for a short squeeze:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>High short interest:</strong> 20%+ of float is shorted</li><li><strong>Low float:</strong> Fewer shares available amplifies the squeeze</li><li><strong>Catalyst:</strong> Positive news, earnings beat, or coordinated buying (like GameStop in 2021)</li><li><strong>Rising price + high volume:</strong> Shorts start covering</li><li><strong>High cost to borrow:</strong> Indicates shares are hard to find</li></ul><p>Track short interest data from sources like Ortex, S3 Partners, or your broker's tools. Finviz shows short interest % for free.</p>" },
      { q: "What is pair trading?", level: "advanced", a: "<p><strong>Pair trading</strong> is a market-neutral strategy that simultaneously goes long on one asset and short on a correlated asset, profiting from the convergence/divergence of the relationship.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> Coca-Cola (KO) and PepsiCo (PEP) typically move together. If KO drops 3% while PEP stays flat (their ratio diverges from the mean), you might long KO and short PEP, expecting the ratio to revert.</p><p><strong>Process:</strong></p><ol><li>Find two correlated instruments (cointegration test)</li><li>Calculate the historical spread or ratio</li><li>When the spread deviates beyond 2 standard deviations, trade the reversion</li><li>Exit when spread returns to mean</li></ol><p>This is market-neutral because you're hedged: if the entire market drops, your short protects your long.</p>" },
      { q: "What is dollar-cost averaging (DCA) and when should I use it?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Dollar-cost averaging</strong> means investing a fixed dollar amount at regular intervals (weekly, monthly) regardless of the current price. This removes the stress of timing the market.</p><p><strong>How it works:</strong> Invest $500/month in an ETF. When prices are high, you buy fewer shares. When prices are low, you buy more shares. Over time, your average cost per share is lower than the average price.</p><p><strong>Research shows:</strong> Lump-sum investing outperforms DCA about 75% of the time because markets tend to rise. But DCA outperforms <em>behaviorally</em> - people are more likely to actually invest consistently than to invest a large lump sum.</p><p><strong>Best for:</strong> Long-term investing (index funds, retirement accounts). Not typically used for active trading.</p>" },
      { q: "What is dividend investing and how do ex-dividend dates work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Dividend investing</strong> focuses on buying stocks that pay regular cash distributions from profits. The goal is building a portfolio that generates passive income.</p><p><strong>Key dates:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Declaration date:</strong> Company announces the dividend amount and payment schedule</li><li><strong>Ex-dividend date:</strong> You must own shares BEFORE this date to receive the dividend. Stock price typically drops by the dividend amount on this date</li><li><strong>Record date:</strong> Company checks who owns shares (1 business day after ex-date)</li><li><strong>Payment date:</strong> Dividend cash hits your account</li></ul><p><strong>Key metrics:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Dividend Yield:</strong> Annual dividend / Stock price. 2-4% is typical for blue chips</li><li><strong>Payout Ratio:</strong> % of earnings paid as dividends. Above 80% may be unsustainable</li><li><strong>Dividend Growth Rate:</strong> How fast dividends are increasing. 'Dividend Aristocrats' have raised dividends for 25+ consecutive years</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is the carry trade in forex?", level: "advanced", a: "<p>The <strong>carry trade</strong> involves borrowing a currency with a low interest rate and investing in one with a higher rate, profiting from the interest rate differential.</p><p><strong>Classic example:</strong> Borrow Japanese Yen at 0.1% interest, convert to Australian Dollars earning 4.35% interest. You earn ~4.25% annually just from the rate difference, plus any appreciation in AUD/JPY.</p><p><strong>Risks:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Currency risk:</strong> If the high-yield currency depreciates more than the interest earned, you lose money</li><li><strong>Carry trade unwind:</strong> During market stress, carry trades unwind rapidly as everyone exits at once, causing massive currency moves (JPY surged 10%+ during the 2024 unwind)</li></ul><p>Carry trades work best in stable, risk-on environments. They blow up during crises.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 28. PORTFOLIO & WEALTH BUILDING ───
  {
    category: "Portfolio & Wealth Building",
    icon: "&#127793;",
    color: "rgba(104,211,145,0.12)",
    description: "ETFs, index funds, and long-term wealth strategies",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is an ETF and how is it different from a mutual fund?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>An <strong>ETF (Exchange-Traded Fund)</strong> holds a basket of securities (stocks, bonds, commodities) and trades on an exchange like a stock. A <strong>mutual fund</strong> also holds a basket of securities but only trades once per day at the closing NAV (Net Asset Value).</p><p><strong>Key differences:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trading:</strong> ETFs trade all day like stocks. Mutual funds only at end-of-day</li><li><strong>Expense ratios:</strong> ETFs are typically cheaper (0.03-0.20% vs. 0.50-1.00%+)</li><li><strong>Tax efficiency:</strong> ETFs are more tax-efficient due to the creation/redemption mechanism</li><li><strong>Minimums:</strong> ETFs have no minimum (buy 1 share). Mutual funds often require $1,000-3,000</li></ul><p>For most investors, low-cost ETFs (like VTI, VOO, QQQ) are the optimal choice.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the expense ratio and why does it matter?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>The <strong>expense ratio</strong> is the annual fee a fund charges as a percentage of your investment. It covers management, administration, and operational costs.</p><p><strong>Impact of fees over time:</strong> On a $100,000 investment earning 8% annually over 30 years:</p><ul><li><strong>0.03% expense ratio (Vanguard VTI):</strong> Grows to ~$988,000. Total fees: ~$9,000</li><li><strong>1.00% expense ratio (typical active fund):</strong> Grows to ~$761,000. Total fees: ~$236,000</li></ul><p>That 0.97% difference costs you <strong>$227,000 over 30 years</strong>. Low expense ratios are one of the most reliable predictors of better fund performance.</p>" },
      { q: "What is index investing and why does it outperform most active managers?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Index investing</strong> means buying a fund that tracks a market index (like the S&P 500) rather than trying to pick individual winning stocks.</p><p><strong>Why it works:</strong> Over 15-year periods, <strong>over 90% of actively managed funds fail to beat their benchmark index</strong> (S&P SPIVA scorecard). After fees, the average active manager destroys value.</p><p><strong>The math:</strong> Markets are a zero-sum game before costs. For every dollar that outperforms, a dollar underperforms. After costs (management fees, trading costs, taxes), the majority of active investors earn less than the index.</p><p>Warren Buffett's famous bet: he wagered $1 million that an S&P 500 index fund would beat a selection of hedge funds over 10 years. The index fund won decisively.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between growth and value investing?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Growth investing:</strong> Buying companies with high earnings growth potential, even at premium valuations. Think tech stocks (Apple, Nvidia, Amazon). Higher potential returns but more volatile. Usually don't pay dividends.</p><p><strong>Value investing:</strong> Buying companies trading below their intrinsic value based on fundamentals (low P/E, high dividend yield). Think Warren Buffett's approach. More stable, often pay dividends, but may underperform in bull markets.</p><p>Historically, value has slightly outperformed growth over very long periods. But growth has dominated since 2010 due to the tech boom. Most advisors recommend owning both via a total market index fund.</p>" },
      { q: "What is asset allocation and the 60/40 portfolio?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Asset allocation</strong> is how you divide your portfolio among different asset classes (stocks, bonds, cash, real estate, alternatives). It's the #1 determinant of long-term returns and risk.</p><p><strong>The 60/40 portfolio:</strong> 60% stocks / 40% bonds. The traditional 'balanced' portfolio. Stocks provide growth; bonds provide stability and income. When stocks drop, bonds traditionally rise, cushioning losses.</p><p><strong>Modern alternatives:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Aggressive (80/20 or 90/10):</strong> More stocks, higher returns, more volatility. Best for young investors with long time horizons</li><li><strong>Conservative (40/60):</strong> More bonds, lower returns, more stability. Better for near-retirees</li><li><strong>All-weather portfolio (Ray Dalio):</strong> 30% stocks, 40% long-term bonds, 15% intermediate bonds, 7.5% gold, 7.5% commodities</li></ul>" },
      { q: "What is compound interest and how does it build wealth?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Compound interest</strong> is earning returns on your returns. It's what Einstein reportedly called 'the eighth wonder of the world.'</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> $10,000 invested at 10% annual return:</p><ul><li>Year 1: $11,000 (+$1,000)</li><li>Year 5: $16,105 (+$6,105)</li><li>Year 10: $25,937 (+$15,937)</li><li>Year 20: $67,275 (+$57,275)</li><li>Year 30: $174,494 (+$164,494)</li></ul><p>The power is in <strong>time</strong>. The difference between starting at age 25 vs. 35 is enormous. Starting 10 years earlier with the same monthly investment can result in 2-3x more wealth at retirement due to compounding.</p>" },
      { q: "What is rebalancing and how often should I do it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Rebalancing</strong> means adjusting your portfolio back to its target allocation as market movements cause drift. If your 60/40 portfolio becomes 70/30 after a stock rally, you sell stocks and buy bonds to restore 60/40.</p><p><strong>Why rebalance:</strong> It maintains your desired risk level and forces you to sell high and buy low (selling the outperformer, buying the underperformer).</p><p><strong>Methods:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Calendar-based:</strong> Rebalance quarterly or annually (simplest)</li><li><strong>Threshold-based:</strong> Rebalance when allocation drifts >5% from target (more responsive)</li><li><strong>Tax-aware:</strong> Use new contributions to rebalance rather than selling (avoids taxable events)</li></ul><p>Research shows annual rebalancing captures most of the benefit. More frequent rebalancing adds transaction costs without meaningfully improving returns.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 29. DAY TRADING SPECIFICS ───
  {
    category: "Day Trading",
    icon: "&#9201;",
    color: "rgba(127,156,245,0.12)",
    description: "Scalping, day trading rules, and intraday strategies",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is day trading and is it right for me?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Day trading</strong> means opening and closing all positions within the same trading day. No overnight holdings.</p><p><strong>Is it right for you?</strong> Consider:</p><ul><li><strong>Capital:</strong> $25,000+ for stocks (PDT rule) or $5,000+ for futures (no PDT)</li><li><strong>Time:</strong> Requires 2-6+ hours of focused screen time during market hours</li><li><strong>Personality:</strong> Quick decision-making, emotional control, comfort with losses</li><li><strong>Risk tolerance:</strong> Most day traders lose money. Statistics suggest only 5-10% are consistently profitable</li><li><strong>Income needs:</strong> Don't day trade if you need consistent income. It takes 1-3 years to develop proficiency</li></ul><p>For most people, swing trading (holding days to weeks) or investing is more practical and has better odds.</p>" },
      { q: "What is scalping and how does it work?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Scalping</strong> is an ultra-short-term strategy where traders aim to profit from tiny price movements, often holding positions for seconds to minutes.</p><p><strong>Characteristics:</strong></p><ul><li>Many trades per day (20-100+)</li><li>Small profit targets (a few ticks to a few points)</li><li>Tight stops (1-3 ticks)</li><li>Requires high liquidity instruments (ES, NQ, EUR/USD, high-volume stocks)</li><li>Low commission costs are essential (fees eat into tiny profits)</li></ul><p><strong>Key tools:</strong> Level 2/DOM (Depth of Market), Time and Sales (tape), footprint charts, 1-minute or tick charts.</p><p>Scalping requires exceptional discipline, fast execution, and the ability to read order flow. It's not recommended for beginners.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the best time of day to trade?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>Different times offer different opportunities:</p><ul><li><strong>9:30 - 10:30 AM ET (Opening Hour):</strong> Highest volatility and volume. Best for momentum/gap strategies. Also most dangerous for beginners</li><li><strong>10:30 AM - 12:00 PM ET:</strong> Volume decreases, trends from open may continue or reverse</li><li><strong>12:00 - 2:00 PM ET (Lunch):</strong> Typically low volume, choppy. Many traders avoid this period</li><li><strong>2:00 - 3:00 PM ET:</strong> Activity picks up, institutional flows increase</li><li><strong>3:00 - 4:00 PM ET (Power Hour):</strong> Strong volume spike. Institutions adjust positions. Trend moves can be powerful</li></ul><p><strong>Beginners:</strong> Start with the opening hour (high setups) or power hour. Avoid lunchtime chop.</p>" },
      { q: "What are the most popular day trading strategies?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>1. Gap and Go:</strong> Trade stocks/futures that gap up/down at open with high volume. Ride the momentum in the direction of the gap.</p><p><strong>2. VWAP Reversion:</strong> Price pulls back to VWAP and bounces. Buy at VWAP in an uptrend, short at VWAP in a downtrend.</p><p><strong>3. Opening Range Breakout (ORB):</strong> Wait for the first 15-30 minutes to establish a range. Trade the breakout above/below with volume confirmation.</p><p><strong>4. ABCD Pattern:</strong> Price action pattern forming a measured move. A-to-B impulse, B-to-C pullback, C-to-D continuation.</p><p><strong>5. Momentum/Breakout:</strong> Trade breakouts above resistance or below support with increasing volume.</p><p>Pick ONE strategy, master it, and only add others when consistently profitable.</p>" },
      { q: "How do I build a pre-market routine for day trading?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A structured pre-market routine separates profitable traders from gamblers:</p><p><strong>30-60 minutes before open:</strong></p><ol><li><strong>Check overnight action:</strong> S&P 500 futures, Asian/European markets, any overnight news</li><li><strong>Review economic calendar:</strong> Any major releases today? (NFP, CPI, FOMC, earnings)</li><li><strong>Scan for gappers:</strong> Which stocks/futures are gapping up or down? Check volume, catalyst, float</li><li><strong>Mark key levels:</strong> Support/resistance, previous day's high/low/close, VWAP, round numbers</li><li><strong>Write your plan:</strong> What setups are you looking for? What are you NOT trading today?</li><li><strong>Set risk parameters:</strong> Max daily loss, max trades, max position size</li><li><strong>Mental check:</strong> Are you focused, rested, and emotionally neutral? If not, consider sitting out</li></ol>" },
      { q: "What is a stock scanner and how do I use one for day trading?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>stock scanner</strong> filters thousands of stocks in real-time based on criteria you define: volume, price change, float, relative volume, sector, etc.</p><p><strong>Common scan criteria for day traders:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Pre-market gappers:</strong> Price change > 4%, volume > 100K shares, price $5-$200</li><li><strong>High relative volume:</strong> Current volume > 2x average (unusual interest)</li><li><strong>52-week highs/lows:</strong> Stocks breaking into new territory</li><li><strong>Unusual options activity:</strong> Large call/put volume relative to average</li></ul><p><strong>Popular scanners:</strong> Trade Ideas (AI-powered, premium), Finviz (free screener), TradingView screener, ThinkorSwim scanner, Benzinga Pro (news + scanner).</p>" },
      { q: "What is the Opening Range Breakout (ORB) strategy?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The <strong>ORB strategy</strong> waits for the first 5, 15, or 30 minutes of trading to establish a range, then trades the breakout above or below that range.</p><p><strong>Setup:</strong></p><ol><li>Mark the high and low of the first 15 or 30 minutes of the session</li><li>If price breaks above the range high with volume, go long. Stop below the range low</li><li>If price breaks below the range low with volume, go short. Stop above the range high</li><li>Target: 1-2x the range size, or hold until a trailing stop is hit</li></ol><p>The ORB works because the opening range captures the battle between overnight positioning and early-morning order flow. A decisive break usually leads to continuation.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 30. PROP FIRM TRADING ───
  {
    category: "Prop Firm Trading (General)",
    icon: "&#127942;",
    color: "rgba(246,173,85,0.12)",
    description: "Funded accounts, evaluations, and prop firm rules",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is a prop firm and how does it work?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>A <strong>prop firm (proprietary trading firm)</strong> provides capital for traders to trade in exchange for a share of the profits. You don't risk your own money (beyond an evaluation fee).</p><p><strong>How it works:</strong></p><ol><li>Pay an evaluation fee ($50-$500+ depending on account size)</li><li>Pass a trading challenge (hit profit target while staying within rules)</li><li>Receive a funded account ($10K to $400K+)</li><li>Trade the funded account and keep 70-90% of profits</li></ol><p><strong>Popular futures prop firms:</strong> Topstep, Apex Trader Funding, Earn2Trade, TradeDay, Take Profit Trader</p><p><strong>Popular forex/stock prop firms:</strong> FTMO, MyFundedFX, The5ers</p>" },
      { q: "What are common prop firm rules and how do I avoid violations?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Typical rules (vary by firm):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Daily loss limit:</strong> Usually 1-3% of account. If hit, trading stops for the day</li><li><strong>Max trailing drawdown:</strong> 3-6% from peak equity. This is the #1 reason traders fail</li><li><strong>Profit target:</strong> 6-10% of account to pass evaluation (varies by firm)</li><li><strong>Minimum trading days:</strong> Often 5-10 days minimum in the evaluation period</li><li><strong>No holding over weekends:</strong> Some firms require flat positions before market close on Friday</li><li><strong>Consistency rule:</strong> No single day's profit can exceed 30-40% of total profit</li></ul><p><strong>How to survive:</strong> Trade small (1-2 contracts even on a $100K account), focus on risk management over profits, and treat the trailing drawdown as your real account balance.</p>" },
      { q: "What is a trailing drawdown and how does it work?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>A <strong>trailing drawdown</strong> is a dynamic loss limit that follows your peak account balance upward but never moves down. It's the most restrictive and commonly failed rule in prop firm evaluations.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> $50K account with $2,500 trailing drawdown (5%). Your stop-out level starts at $47,500. If your account grows to $52,000, your stop-out moves to $49,500 ($52,000 - $2,500). If account drops to $49,500, you're done.</p><p><strong>Key insight:</strong> The trailing drawdown means you can't give back your profits. A $500 winning trade that you then give back is WORSE than never having taken it (because the drawdown followed you up).</p><div class='tip'>Some firms trail drawdown only during trading hours (end-of-day). Others trail in real-time (intraday). The real-time version is much harder. Always check which type your firm uses.</div>" },
      { q: "What is the best strategy for passing a prop firm evaluation?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>The goal is NOT to be the most profitable trader. It's to reach the profit target without violating any rules. Think <strong>defense over offense</strong>.</p><p><strong>Winning approach:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Trade small:</strong> Use 1/3 to 1/2 of the allowed maximum position size</li><li><strong>Aim for singles:</strong> Don't try to hit the profit target in one day. Target 0.5-1% of account per day</li><li><strong>Set a daily max loss:</strong> Limit yourself to 1/3 of the drawdown limit per day</li><li><strong>Trade your best setup only:</strong> 1-3 trades per day max. Quality over quantity</li><li><strong>Never revenge trade:</strong> If you hit your daily max loss, walk away immediately</li><li><strong>Use bracket orders:</strong> Pre-set stops and targets so emotions can't interfere</li></ul>" },
      { q: "Can I use automated trading with prop firms?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p>Most prop firms <strong>allow automated trading</strong>, but with restrictions:</p><ul><li><strong>Topstep:</strong> Allows automation via supported platforms (NinjaTrader, TradingView with webhooks)</li><li><strong>Apex Trader Funding:</strong> Allows automation, popular with TradingView + PickMyTrade users</li><li><strong>FTMO:</strong> Allows Expert Advisors (EAs) on MetaTrader</li></ul><p><strong>Things to verify:</strong></p><ul><li>Does the firm support your platform/broker?</li><li>Are there restrictions on trade frequency or holding time?</li><li>Does the firm's consistency rule affect your bot's trading style?</li><li>Can your bot respect the daily loss limit and drawdown rules?</li></ul><p>Automation can actually help with prop firm evaluations by removing emotional decision-making and enforcing disciplined risk management. PickMyTrade is specifically designed to work with prop firm accounts across Tradovate, Rithmic, and other supported platforms.</p>" }
    ]
  },

  // ─── 31. MARKET DATA & TOOLS ───
  {
    category: "Market Data & Tools",
    icon: "&#128295;",
    color: "rgba(99,179,237,0.12)",
    description: "Platforms, data sources, and essential trading tools",
    questions: [
      { q: "What is TradingView and why is it so popular?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>TradingView</strong> is the world's most popular charting platform and social network for traders. It offers:</p><ul><li>Advanced charting with 100+ built-in indicators</li><li><strong>Pine Script:</strong> Custom indicator/strategy programming language</li><li><strong>Alerts:</strong> Price, indicator, and strategy alerts via email, app, or webhook</li><li><strong>Social features:</strong> Share ideas, follow other traders, comment on charts</li><li><strong>Screener:</strong> Stock, forex, and crypto screening tools</li><li>Works in any browser - no software to install</li></ul><p><strong>Plans:</strong> Free (limited features), Pro ($12.95/mo), Pro+ ($24.95/mo), Premium ($49.95/mo). Webhook alerts require Pro or above.</p>" },
      { q: "What is Level 2 data and do I need it?", level: "intermediate", a: "<p><strong>Level 2 data</strong> (also called Depth of Market or DOM) shows the full order book - every buy and sell order at each price level with the size and the market maker posting it.</p><p><strong>Level 1:</strong> Best bid and ask (what you see by default)</p><p><strong>Level 2:</strong> All bids and asks at every price level, showing the 'depth' of supply and demand</p><p><strong>Who needs it:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Scalpers/Day traders:</strong> Yes. Reading order flow is essential for very short-term trading</li><li><strong>Swing/Position traders:</strong> Not really. Level 1 plus good charting is sufficient</li><li><strong>Options traders:</strong> Helpful but not essential</li></ul><p><strong>Caveat:</strong> About 35-45% of volume executes in dark pools and is invisible to Level 2. The order book you see is an incomplete picture.</p>" },
      { q: "What economic calendar should I use?", level: "beginner", a: "<p>An <strong>economic calendar</strong> tracks scheduled data releases, central bank meetings, and events that move markets. Checking it daily is essential to avoid being blindsided by news.</p><p><strong>Free calendars:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Forex Factory:</strong> Best for forex traders. Color-coded impact levels, historical data</li><li><strong>Investing.com:</strong> Comprehensive coverage across all markets</li><li><strong>TradingView Economic Calendar:</strong> Integrated into TradingView platform</li><li><strong>CME FedWatch Tool:</strong> Specifically tracks Fed rate expectations</li></ul><p><strong>Daily habit:</strong> Before you start trading each day, check the economic calendar. Note any high-impact events and their scheduled times. Either avoid trading during those periods or prepare for increased volatility.</p>" },
      { q: "What is the difference between real-time and delayed data?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Real-time data:</strong> Prices update instantly as trades occur. Essential for day trading and scalping. May require a monthly data feed subscription ($10-30/month per exchange).</p><p><strong>Delayed data:</strong> Prices are typically 15-20 minutes behind. Free on most platforms. Adequate for swing traders, investors, and analysis/education.</p><p><strong>Data feed providers:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Exchange direct:</strong> CME (futures), NYSE/NASDAQ (stocks) - most accurate</li><li><strong>Broker-provided:</strong> Often included free with an account</li><li><strong>Third-party:</strong> CQG, Rithmic, dxFeed (used by trading platforms)</li></ul><p>If you're day trading, real-time data is non-negotiable. Even a 1-second delay can mean entering or exiting at significantly different prices.</p>" },
      { q: "What charting timeframes should I use?", level: "beginner", a: "<p><strong>Multiple timeframe analysis</strong> is key. Use a higher timeframe for trend direction and a lower timeframe for entries:</p><p><strong>Scalping:</strong></p><ul><li>Trend: 5-minute chart</li><li>Entry: 1-minute or tick chart</li></ul><p><strong>Day Trading:</strong></p><ul><li>Trend: Daily + 1-hour chart</li><li>Entry: 5-15 minute chart</li></ul><p><strong>Swing Trading:</strong></p><ul><li>Trend: Weekly + Daily chart</li><li>Entry: 4-hour or Daily chart</li></ul><p><strong>Position Trading/Investing:</strong></p><ul><li>Trend: Monthly + Weekly chart</li><li>Entry: Daily chart</li></ul><p>General rule: analyze on a timeframe 3-5x higher than your entry timeframe. This prevents trading against the bigger trend.</p>" }
    ]
  },

